WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:16.230
PRESENTER: CBBS, which is the
Center for Bicultural Bilingual

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:19.740
Studies at MIT, has kindly
asked me to introduce Professor

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:21.860
Hagege to you this afternoon.

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:26.400
And I feel extremely honored
and privileged to do so.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:29.790
So some of you may have
listened to him last night.

00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:32.759
And if you have,
and have come back,

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:36.480
it's because you know that
you are in for a treat again.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:39.840
And if you have not yet
listened to him and heard him,

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:43.620
you will soon find out how
fortunate you are to be here

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:47.760
and how fortunate we are to
have Professor Hagege speak

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:49.570
to us on campus.

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:52.280
But first let me say just
a few words about him.

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:56.240
He's an internationally
renowned linguist who

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:58.430
was born and raised in Tunis.

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:01.670
And ever since he was a
child, he told us yesterday,

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:03.500
he was drawn to languages.

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:05.510
And he himself speaks--

00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:07.550
I just asked him how
many languages he spoke,

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:08.990
and he told me, I don't know.

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:10.190
Haven't counted them.

00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:12.730
So anyway, at
least 10, maybe 12.

00:01:12.730 --> 00:01:13.240
Who knows?

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:15.380
But many.

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:16.940
Many.

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:19.010
So he has written many,
many books and articles

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:22.820
ranging from very
scholarly works, two about

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:26.480
generative grammar, two works
on the origins of languages

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:30.140
as well as the social and
cultural impact of language.

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:33.620
And all this being the result
of a tireless combination

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:36.200
of theoretical
research and fieldwork.

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:40.320
And Professor Hagege is
a man who never tires,

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:43.520
he tells us, and never rests.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:48.260
And since 1988 he has
titulaire de la chaire

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:51.680
de theorie linguistique
au College de France.

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:55.460
And he has received many,
many awards, among which,

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:59.690
in 1995, the Medaille
d'Or du CNRS,

00:01:59.690 --> 00:02:04.430
which is a French award given
to people who have given only

00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:07.850
to people who have contributed
exceptionally to research

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:11.787
and [INAUDIBLE] accomplishments.

00:02:11.787 --> 00:02:13.370
And of course, the
number of you here,

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:16.200
the number of people here,
is also a testament to that

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:18.100
as well.

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:23.100
Plus, you will discover, as
something you may not yet know,

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:27.840
that Professor Hagege, on
top of everything else,

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:31.550
is a thoroughly delightful man.

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:36.630
So his most recent book, a
widely acclaimed one which is

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:40.230
entitled Halte a la morte
des langues, and which is,

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:42.630
by the way, about to be
translated in English

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:46.510
and published by Stanford
University Press,

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:51.380
is a [FRENCH],, is a call
for action, if you will,

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:55.620
a wake up call about
the catastrophic--

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:57.930
at least that's his
own word-- impact

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:01.440
the disappearance
of languages has

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:05.550
on our overall human, social,
cultural, and intellectual

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:07.570
heritage.

00:03:07.570 --> 00:03:12.270
His topic afternoon is English
As a Global Language: A Threat

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:13.620
or Myth?

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:18.030
It's a topic that no doubt
arouses quite a bit of interest

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:20.790
in those who feel
sometimes passionately,

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:22.890
the way the French
do in particular,

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:25.530
that their own language is being
threatened by what is often

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:30.030
perceived as the linguistic and
cultural imperialism, quote,

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:30.890
of English.

00:03:30.890 --> 00:03:33.510
I say quote because this is a
word that Professor Hagege uses

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:36.090
himself, and I hope you don't
think it's too polemic a word

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:37.920
to use, but you will tell us.

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:41.390
Anyway, and I suspect that this
is a topic that most of you

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:43.910
here are interested
in, since you are here.

00:03:43.910 --> 00:03:46.150
Anyway, in his book Halte
a la morte des langues,

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:48.442
Professor Hagege
writes, and I quote,

00:03:48.442 --> 00:03:50.150
in French first, then
English, [FRENCH]..

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:15.370
Feebly translated, it might
be useful to ask oneself what

00:04:15.370 --> 00:04:18.040
is lost when languages die.

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:21.290
The part of genius that
is deposited within each

00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:25.780
of the languages is large
enough that when some die,

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:31.030
it can be called catastrophic,
and that what disappears

00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:36.920
is forever lost to our
universal well of humanity.

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:39.550
So it is about this
[INAUDIBLE] relationship

00:04:39.550 --> 00:04:44.092
between language, culture,
and humanity that Mr. Claude

00:04:44.092 --> 00:04:46.550
Hagege, Professor Claude Hagege,
will to speak to us today.

00:04:46.550 --> 00:04:48.230
And within the
framework of the topic,

00:04:48.230 --> 00:04:50.510
is English as a global
language threat--

00:04:55.210 --> 00:04:56.882
threat or myth?

00:04:56.882 --> 00:04:57.699
Is that right?

00:04:57.699 --> 00:04:58.240
AUDIENCE: No.

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:00.070
[INAUDIBLE]

00:05:00.070 --> 00:05:01.040
PRESENTER: OK.

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:03.700
English as Global Language:
Real or Imagined Threat?

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:04.260
I'm sorry.

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:07.562
[INAUDIBLE] So he will speak
for about an hour at most.

00:05:07.562 --> 00:05:08.520
HAGEGE: I would say so.

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:09.220
That's fine.

00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:10.760
PRESENTER: And since he
speaks so many languages,

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:13.010
maybe you could ask some of
these questions in Arabic,

00:05:13.010 --> 00:05:13.890
Russian.

00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:14.390
HAGEGE: Yes.

00:05:14.390 --> 00:05:16.030
I will reply to
any question asked

00:05:16.030 --> 00:05:21.230
in Chinese, Arabic,
Russian, Spanish, Italian,

00:05:21.230 --> 00:05:23.626
except English.

00:05:23.626 --> 00:05:25.250
PRESENTER: Well,
anyway it is high time

00:05:25.250 --> 00:05:29.199
that [SPEAKING FRENCH]
Professor Hagege.

00:05:29.199 --> 00:05:30.740
HAGEGE: Before
starting, with respect

00:05:30.740 --> 00:05:33.710
to the question you asked, I
did not answer immediately.

00:05:33.710 --> 00:05:36.680
But I reflected, I
thought of your question,

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:38.592
and now I have a kind of reply.

00:05:38.592 --> 00:05:39.800
PRESENTER: To which question?

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:41.341
HAGEGE: How many
languages you speak.

00:05:41.341 --> 00:05:43.970
Well, so in order to answer this
question of how many languages

00:05:43.970 --> 00:05:49.530
I speak, I think of my
old rabbi in my childhood.

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:52.124
He said, when someone
you don't know,

00:05:52.124 --> 00:05:54.290
someone who might be a foe--
which is not your case,

00:05:54.290 --> 00:05:55.690
by any means--

00:05:55.690 --> 00:05:58.490
asks you a question, he
said with a quite Jewish,

00:05:58.490 --> 00:06:02.850
Yiddish intonation, answer
with another question.

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:05.930
So since you asked that
question, I will answer what

00:06:05.930 --> 00:06:08.360
do you mean by
speaking a language?

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:12.500
If you mean how many languages
do you write, do you give talks

00:06:12.500 --> 00:06:17.330
in, do you use to write books
without necessarily speaking

00:06:17.330 --> 00:06:19.430
them, and if you
mean languages do

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:22.790
you use when you are
in love of someone,

00:06:22.790 --> 00:06:24.290
the answer will
vary according to

00:06:24.290 --> 00:06:25.670
these different circumstances.

00:06:25.670 --> 00:06:26.545
PRESENTER: Thank you.

00:06:33.737 --> 00:06:35.320
HAGEGE: What's the
title of this talk?

00:06:38.485 --> 00:06:41.400
PRESENTER: Are you serious?

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:42.520
HAGEGE: Partly.

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:43.430
Thank you.

00:06:43.430 --> 00:06:43.930
Oh, yes.

00:06:46.974 --> 00:06:48.140
I might make the wrong talk.

00:06:53.420 --> 00:06:56.390
Whether we monitor the veritable
army of English speaking

00:06:56.390 --> 00:07:00.170
econo-technical or specialist
advisors and representatives,

00:07:00.170 --> 00:07:01.750
or whether we examine
the [INAUDIBLE]

00:07:01.750 --> 00:07:04.940
of English publications,
films, radio and television

00:07:04.940 --> 00:07:09.140
programs, literacy programs,
and educational opportunities,

00:07:09.140 --> 00:07:11.270
it is becoming
increasingly clear

00:07:11.270 --> 00:07:13.640
that non-English
mother tongue countries

00:07:13.640 --> 00:07:17.390
are significantly active in
each of these connections.

00:07:17.390 --> 00:07:20.990
Nor is their involvement merely
that of third world recipients

00:07:20.990 --> 00:07:22.820
of Western largesse.

00:07:22.820 --> 00:07:25.340
True third world
nations are themselves

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:30.140
fostering massive efforts
via and on behalf of English.

00:07:30.140 --> 00:07:33.800
On the other hand, however,
equally massive programs via

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:36.920
English are being conducted
by the Soviet Union--

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:38.540
by the Russians, let us say--

00:07:38.540 --> 00:07:41.210
the Arab world,
and mainland China,

00:07:41.210 --> 00:07:44.090
world powers that have their
own well-developed standard

00:07:44.090 --> 00:07:47.660
languages and that normally
oppose various political,

00:07:47.660 --> 00:07:50.750
philosophical, and economic
goals of the English mother

00:07:50.750 --> 00:07:52.400
tongue worlds.

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:55.670
Whereas the international and
intranational rules of French

00:07:55.670 --> 00:08:00.120
also continue to be fostered,
as do, to a lesser degree,

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:03.360
such rules for Spanish,
Russian, German, Portuguese,

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:06.620
and so forth, such efforts
are conducted exclusively

00:08:06.620 --> 00:08:09.260
by current francophone,
hispanohablantes,

00:08:09.260 --> 00:08:12.660
and so on nations, or by
countries under French,

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:15.830
Spanish, and so on cultural,
political, or economic

00:08:15.830 --> 00:08:17.300
domination.

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:20.810
Similarly, English is
massively employed particularly

00:08:20.810 --> 00:08:23.840
in higher level governmental
technological and educational

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:26.720
pursuits by countries
under former

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:30.050
or current Anglo-American
domination.

00:08:30.050 --> 00:08:34.280
However, English today has
surpassed the charmed circle

00:08:34.280 --> 00:08:38.210
of Anglo-American
econo-political control,

00:08:38.210 --> 00:08:40.730
and is being fostered
both by its opponents

00:08:40.730 --> 00:08:42.710
and by its third parties.

00:08:42.710 --> 00:08:44.810
English has become
a major medium

00:08:44.810 --> 00:08:48.380
of indigenous elites, native
foreigners, of tourism,

00:08:48.380 --> 00:08:51.710
foreign foreigners,
of popular media,

00:08:51.710 --> 00:08:55.460
of technical publications,
of the metaphor of mastery,

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:58.130
of teenage slang,
and even of language

00:08:58.130 --> 00:09:02.040
planning models and anti-models,
all of other worlds.

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:03.740
While the omnipresence
of English

00:09:03.740 --> 00:09:06.230
also adds to the
opposition to English,

00:09:06.230 --> 00:09:09.950
it obviously fosters the
growth of indigenous non-native

00:09:09.950 --> 00:09:11.390
varieties.

00:09:11.390 --> 00:09:14.450
Finally, never before
has any one language

00:09:14.450 --> 00:09:19.310
been so simultaneously sought
after and regulated so that it

00:09:19.310 --> 00:09:23.210
would grow yet stay
in its place, that is,

00:09:23.210 --> 00:09:25.610
be used only in
functions for which it

00:09:25.610 --> 00:09:28.370
was authoritatively desired.

00:09:28.370 --> 00:09:31.340
Thus, if the continued
spread and growth of English

00:09:31.340 --> 00:09:33.980
is one aspect of the current
international social and

00:09:33.980 --> 00:09:37.640
linguistic balance of
power, another such aspect

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:41.567
is the recurring need to
control, regulate, or tame

00:09:41.567 --> 00:09:42.150
their spreads.

00:09:48.370 --> 00:09:52.420
We more or less
consider that today,

00:09:52.420 --> 00:09:55.850
whatever the total number,
non-native users of English

00:09:55.850 --> 00:09:59.410
outnumber the native users.

00:09:59.410 --> 00:10:01.720
The current sociolinguistic
profile of English

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:04.960
may be viewed in terms
of three circles, more

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:07.250
or less concentric circles.

00:10:07.250 --> 00:10:10.540
The so-called inner
circle, as proposed

00:10:10.540 --> 00:10:14.410
by [INAUDIBLE] in research
he did on this topic,

00:10:14.410 --> 00:10:17.110
refers to the traditional
cultural and linguistic basis

00:10:17.110 --> 00:10:17.950
of English.

00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:22.780
It is made of either USA, the
UK, Canada, Australia, New

00:10:22.780 --> 00:10:23.950
Zealand.

00:10:23.950 --> 00:10:30.090
Then we have a
outer circle, which

00:10:30.090 --> 00:10:33.840
is made of Bangladesh, Ghana,
India, Kenya, Malaysia,

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:37.140
Nigeria, Pakistan,
Philippines, Singapore, Sri

00:10:37.140 --> 00:10:39.690
Lanka, Tanzania, Zambia.

00:10:39.690 --> 00:10:43.170
And finally, what we call
the expanding circle,

00:10:43.170 --> 00:10:47.530
which contains China, Egypt,
Indonesia, Israel, Japan,

00:10:47.530 --> 00:10:53.750
Korea, Nepal Saudi Arabia,
Taiwan, Russia, Zimbabwe.

00:10:53.750 --> 00:10:56.360
The numerical profile
is impressive indeed.

00:10:56.360 --> 00:10:59.360
Even if we use a
conservative estimates

00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:04.100
of the use of English, that
is, approximately 800 millions,

00:11:04.100 --> 00:11:08.210
about 57% are non-native users.

00:11:08.210 --> 00:11:11.760
The optimistic figure of two
billion users, of course,

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:14.360
increases the percentage
significantly.

00:11:14.360 --> 00:11:18.050
However, the term users is
rather tricky, particularly

00:11:18.050 --> 00:11:20.090
in the non-native context.

00:11:20.090 --> 00:11:22.160
The vagueness of
its use is somewhat

00:11:22.160 --> 00:11:25.670
reduced by restricting
it to educated speakers,

00:11:25.670 --> 00:11:27.470
though by the use
of the term two,

00:11:27.470 --> 00:11:30.410
we are opening a can of worms.

00:11:30.410 --> 00:11:32.930
The result of this
spread is that formally

00:11:32.930 --> 00:11:37.730
and functionally, English now
has multicultural identities.

00:11:37.730 --> 00:11:39.440
The term English
does not capture

00:11:39.440 --> 00:11:41.210
the sociolinguistic reality.

00:11:41.210 --> 00:11:44.060
I will propose later another
term which probably does.

00:11:48.780 --> 00:11:54.810
Such former colonies as
Bangladesh, Botswana, Brunei,

00:11:54.810 --> 00:11:57.270
British colonies,
Burma, Cameroon--

00:11:57.270 --> 00:11:58.800
British and French--

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:02.700
Ethiopia, Fiji, Gambia, Ghana,
India, Israel, Kenya, Lesotho,

00:12:02.700 --> 00:12:08.700
Liberia, and many others are
among the members of the Anglo

00:12:08.700 --> 00:12:11.760
English speaking world today,
and of course they contribute

00:12:11.760 --> 00:12:12.980
to increase this number.

00:12:16.310 --> 00:12:19.870
What are the phases of
spread of English today

00:12:19.870 --> 00:12:22.380
in the contemporary world?

00:12:22.380 --> 00:12:27.850
Now after having studied
the situation, and what

00:12:27.850 --> 00:12:33.460
I call the three circles, I am
studying the phases of spread.

00:12:33.460 --> 00:12:35.210
The English language
and its literature

00:12:35.210 --> 00:12:39.580
moved toward multiplicity in
three broad sweeps to, first,

00:12:39.580 --> 00:12:43.520
Scotland, Wales, and Ireland;
second, North America,

00:12:43.520 --> 00:12:46.270
Australia, New Zealand,
and South Africa;

00:12:46.270 --> 00:12:50.260
and third, Asia, Africa, the
West Indies, the Pacific,

00:12:50.260 --> 00:12:52.840
and other geographical pockets.

00:12:52.840 --> 00:12:56.770
Important for my present
purpose in this rough chronology

00:12:56.770 --> 00:13:00.460
of some 500 years are
the generalized factors

00:13:00.460 --> 00:13:02.530
distinguishing each movement.

00:13:02.530 --> 00:13:06.670
In the first, the language
spread by arms, politics,

00:13:06.670 --> 00:13:10.570
and culture as part of
an assimilative process

00:13:10.570 --> 00:13:14.650
through rearranged
fiefdoms, principalities,

00:13:14.650 --> 00:13:17.460
and kingdoms of
Anglo-Saxon Norman

00:13:17.460 --> 00:13:19.690
hegemonies over the Celts.

00:13:19.690 --> 00:13:21.370
The Irish, for
instance, have hardly

00:13:21.370 --> 00:13:23.950
had difficulty with
the English language,

00:13:23.950 --> 00:13:26.230
only with the English regime.

00:13:26.230 --> 00:13:29.110
And lest we forget, at
the setting of the sun,

00:13:29.110 --> 00:13:32.800
the greatest English
wits have been Irish.

00:13:32.800 --> 00:13:35.320
Moreover, many of
them live today

00:13:35.320 --> 00:13:37.660
in the America, or
their descendants.

00:13:37.660 --> 00:13:40.990
The difference were part of a
symbiotic relationship arising

00:13:40.990 --> 00:13:43.660
from a large measure
of shared culture,

00:13:43.660 --> 00:13:46.330
if not of shared politics.

00:13:46.330 --> 00:13:49.150
In the second movement,
language and culture spread

00:13:49.150 --> 00:13:51.160
as English speakers spread.

00:13:51.160 --> 00:13:54.250
Major institutions of
identity were transferred,

00:13:54.250 --> 00:13:56.590
at times replicated, and grew.

00:13:56.590 --> 00:14:01.030
Strong, constant contact with
England, at times paradoxical,

00:14:01.030 --> 00:14:04.870
maintained bonds that survived
such varied and chronologically

00:14:04.870 --> 00:14:08.050
separate happenings as the
American War of Independence,

00:14:08.050 --> 00:14:11.350
the Boer War in South Africa,
the reaction in Australia

00:14:11.350 --> 00:14:15.000
and New Zealand when Britain
joined the EEC very recently,

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:17.590
and South Africa's expulsion
from the Commonwealth,

00:14:17.590 --> 00:14:18.385
also very recently.

00:14:25.060 --> 00:14:30.160
Now if I try to reach
a kind of assessment

00:14:30.160 --> 00:14:32.800
by comparing the
progress of English

00:14:32.800 --> 00:14:35.410
with the progress of other
cultures in the past,

00:14:35.410 --> 00:14:38.330
let me mention the case
of Greek and Latin.

00:14:38.330 --> 00:14:40.050
Greek, in the
period of Hellenism,

00:14:40.050 --> 00:14:42.680
carried the culture
of intellectualism

00:14:42.680 --> 00:14:46.630
in science, philosophy,
and art and became a must

00:14:46.630 --> 00:14:50.020
for the educated from
Rome to Asia Minor.

00:14:50.020 --> 00:14:53.590
Latin is resuscitated in
the Carolingian period

00:14:53.590 --> 00:14:58.030
in the eighth and ninth
centuries for the necessities

00:14:58.030 --> 00:15:00.910
of ecclesiastic and
mundane administration,

00:15:00.910 --> 00:15:02.860
and for seven or eight
centuries, remains

00:15:02.860 --> 00:15:07.860
the vehicle of administration
and of written communication

00:15:07.860 --> 00:15:09.150
in the Western world.

00:15:09.150 --> 00:15:12.330
Their chivalric culture
of medieval aristocracy,

00:15:12.330 --> 00:15:16.320
rising in Provence in France,
carries these languages often

00:15:16.320 --> 00:15:20.280
not clearly separable over
the Western world and beyond,

00:15:20.280 --> 00:15:23.190
from England to the
Crusader states.

00:15:23.190 --> 00:15:26.570
Renaissance Italy brings
us into the modern world

00:15:26.570 --> 00:15:30.540
with the educated culture who
combines the two traditions

00:15:30.540 --> 00:15:32.640
of the humanist and the knight.

00:15:32.640 --> 00:15:35.800
Italian is part
of this equipment.

00:15:35.800 --> 00:15:39.030
And French, again, in the
18th and 19th centuries,

00:15:39.030 --> 00:15:41.880
spreads through the
innumerable courts modeled

00:15:41.880 --> 00:15:44.460
after Versailles, and
the language of the court

00:15:44.460 --> 00:15:47.310
survives as the language
of international diplomacy,

00:15:47.310 --> 00:15:49.530
sifting down into
the bourgeoisie

00:15:49.530 --> 00:15:52.920
and remaining a distinctive
mark of that bourgeoisie

00:15:52.920 --> 00:15:55.830
far beyond Europe in
Latin America, the Middle

00:15:55.830 --> 00:15:58.400
East, and Africa.

00:15:58.400 --> 00:16:02.720
Ours is a day, as opposed to
all that, of American English.

00:16:02.720 --> 00:16:07.730
And you may know
that as early as 780,

00:16:07.730 --> 00:16:10.970
John Adams made a
remarkable statement.

00:16:10.970 --> 00:16:14.450
He proved to be prophetic
when he said English--

00:16:14.450 --> 00:16:16.010
that is, American English--

00:16:16.010 --> 00:16:19.120
is destined to be in the next
and succeeding centuries--

00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:21.290
the next, for him, was
the 19th, and succeeded

00:16:21.290 --> 00:16:23.810
by the 20th and our century.

00:16:23.810 --> 00:16:27.170
So he said English is
destined to be in the next

00:16:27.170 --> 00:16:30.020
and succeeding centuries,
more generally,

00:16:30.020 --> 00:16:32.520
the language of the world
than Latin was in the past

00:16:32.520 --> 00:16:35.420
or France is in the
present age when he wrote

00:16:35.420 --> 00:16:37.650
in the end of the 18th century.

00:16:37.650 --> 00:16:40.790
The reason, he said, is
obvious, because the increasing

00:16:40.790 --> 00:16:44.180
population of America and
the universal connection

00:16:44.180 --> 00:16:46.370
and correspondence
with all nations

00:16:46.370 --> 00:16:49.190
forced their language
into general use.

00:16:49.190 --> 00:16:51.110
What is remarkable
in Adam's statement

00:16:51.110 --> 00:16:54.440
is his perceptions, at
such a pristine stage,

00:16:54.440 --> 00:16:56.780
of the history of the country.

00:16:56.780 --> 00:17:00.260
Indeed, in 1780,
American English

00:17:00.260 --> 00:17:04.500
is still the underdog, still
a colonial substandard.

00:17:04.500 --> 00:17:07.170
The colonial regime
reaches its end,

00:17:07.170 --> 00:17:09.890
but the linguistic class
system is still vigorous.

00:17:09.890 --> 00:17:15.079
A gentleman, says Princeton's
President Witherspoon in 1781,

00:17:15.079 --> 00:17:17.060
will not imitate a peasant.

00:17:17.060 --> 00:17:19.579
In this diglossia-- diglossia,
which is not the same

00:17:19.579 --> 00:17:20.750
as bilingualism--

00:17:20.750 --> 00:17:23.420
British English is the
high language, the prestige

00:17:23.420 --> 00:17:25.310
language, and
American language is

00:17:25.310 --> 00:17:27.950
the non-prestigious
low language.

00:17:27.950 --> 00:17:32.660
Loyalty to the British tradition
means, linguistically, purism.

00:17:32.660 --> 00:17:37.910
Now, are British English
and American English

00:17:37.910 --> 00:17:41.670
really different
from each other?

00:17:41.670 --> 00:17:43.880
The story of spoken
American English,

00:17:43.880 --> 00:17:45.500
of the American
colony, is indeed

00:17:45.500 --> 00:17:47.960
one of linguistic
democratization.

00:17:47.960 --> 00:17:52.790
It has been in existence
from before 1700,

00:17:52.790 --> 00:17:55.880
and it survived, in
principle, into our days

00:17:55.880 --> 00:17:58.130
in the prestige accorded
to our relatively

00:17:58.130 --> 00:18:00.650
integrated American vernacular.

00:18:00.650 --> 00:18:02.890
I speak as if I were
myself an American, which

00:18:02.890 --> 00:18:05.090
is not the case by any means.

00:18:05.090 --> 00:18:08.900
Dr. Benjamin Rush, signer of
the Declaration of Independence,

00:18:08.900 --> 00:18:11.280
rejects the British paradigm.

00:18:11.280 --> 00:18:14.480
He writes, "The present
is the age of simplicity

00:18:14.480 --> 00:18:16.100
of writing in America."

00:18:16.100 --> 00:18:18.650
And Noah Webster,
well-known Noah Webster,

00:18:18.650 --> 00:18:22.560
to whom language was national,
as national as customs, habits,

00:18:22.560 --> 00:18:26.980
and governments,
sensed early in 1789

00:18:26.980 --> 00:18:28.940
a divergence of the
language of North America

00:18:28.940 --> 00:18:30.950
from the future
language of England.

00:18:30.950 --> 00:18:33.920
Webster was, so to
speak, the discoverer

00:18:33.920 --> 00:18:37.160
of the national language
of the United States.

00:18:37.160 --> 00:18:40.310
Now to what extent
can we say or maintain

00:18:40.310 --> 00:18:43.520
that British English and
American English are different?

00:18:43.520 --> 00:18:46.130
Linguistically speaking,
these two variants

00:18:46.130 --> 00:18:47.870
of English, British
and American,

00:18:47.870 --> 00:18:50.890
are far more similar
than they are different.

00:18:50.890 --> 00:18:54.560
While BE, British English,
and AE, American English, each

00:18:54.560 --> 00:18:58.100
possesses its own range of
local dialectical variants,

00:18:58.100 --> 00:19:00.620
educated usage is by
and large sufficiently

00:19:00.620 --> 00:19:03.230
similar for easy
intercommunication.

00:19:03.230 --> 00:19:06.740
At the level of grammar, there
are remarkably few differences

00:19:06.740 --> 00:19:10.820
of consequence, though there are
a number of trivial differences

00:19:10.820 --> 00:19:14.780
of the type in hospital,
in British English,

00:19:14.780 --> 00:19:17.210
in the hospital, in
American English.

00:19:17.210 --> 00:19:20.990
The book will be published
on Friday in British,

00:19:20.990 --> 00:19:23.570
and it will be published
Friday in American.

00:19:23.570 --> 00:19:24.560
I suppose so, at least.

00:19:24.560 --> 00:19:28.760
I don't know if this is
your collected usage.

00:19:28.760 --> 00:19:32.120
Again, American English, I
already had my breakfast.

00:19:32.120 --> 00:19:36.440
British English, I've already
had or got my breakfast, which

00:19:36.440 --> 00:19:38.120
I must confess, to
me, as a European,

00:19:38.120 --> 00:19:41.450
is much more elegant
than American English.

00:19:41.450 --> 00:19:43.610
Or do you have your
passport with you?

00:19:43.610 --> 00:19:45.620
Yes I do, in American English.

00:19:45.620 --> 00:19:50.720
British English, have you
got your passport with you?

00:19:50.720 --> 00:19:52.040
Yes, I have.

00:19:52.040 --> 00:19:53.700
And so forth.

00:19:53.700 --> 00:19:56.000
These are minor
differences, I think.

00:19:56.000 --> 00:20:01.010
They cannot lead us to
decide that there is a great

00:20:01.010 --> 00:20:02.960
difference between
the two norms,

00:20:02.960 --> 00:20:04.670
the British and
the American norm.

00:20:04.670 --> 00:20:08.190
As far as phonetic
pronunciation is concerned,

00:20:08.190 --> 00:20:09.650
we have differences.

00:20:09.650 --> 00:20:13.370
I must say that I, as a
European, a Frenchman,

00:20:13.370 --> 00:20:16.310
having, as you can see,
a very pro-English,

00:20:16.310 --> 00:20:18.590
did not understand the
Americans at the beginning.

00:20:18.590 --> 00:20:21.740
First time when I met
some, when they pronounced

00:20:21.740 --> 00:20:24.770
intervocalic t's, I mean
t between two vowels,

00:20:24.770 --> 00:20:28.160
because when I heard
the Americans saying

00:20:28.160 --> 00:20:32.570
a man or a woman who writes,
a person who is waiting,

00:20:32.570 --> 00:20:37.430
for a thing I am referring to,
I heard a writer, [INAUDIBLE]

00:20:37.430 --> 00:20:42.290
matter exactly as if they were
speaking of a rider, a wedding,

00:20:42.290 --> 00:20:44.030
and a matter.

00:20:44.030 --> 00:20:47.830
Of course, the pronunciation
is in fact not a D,

00:20:47.830 --> 00:20:51.455
but what we call in our
phonetic terms a one flap

00:20:51.455 --> 00:20:53.150
R. The pronunciation--

00:20:53.150 --> 00:20:53.720
I suppose.

00:20:53.720 --> 00:20:54.261
I don't know.

00:20:54.261 --> 00:20:56.140
I can't imitate
it the right way.

00:20:56.140 --> 00:20:59.420
But I suppose the pronunciation
of pretty, a [INAUDIBLE],,

00:20:59.420 --> 00:21:02.504
is pretty, pretty with
a [INAUDIBLE],, pretty.

00:21:02.504 --> 00:21:04.670
I must confess-- excuse me
for this aggressiveness--

00:21:04.670 --> 00:21:06.860
that this sounds extremely
vulgar to my ear.

00:21:06.860 --> 00:21:11.270
Pretty, which is British, is the
right pronunciation, of course.

00:21:11.270 --> 00:21:14.390
Well, it's the British one.

00:21:14.390 --> 00:21:20.300
So we cannot say that we have
here two different languages.

00:21:20.300 --> 00:21:22.790
At the maximum we
have two norms.

00:21:22.790 --> 00:21:28.310
Although there have been
many, many studies devoted

00:21:28.310 --> 00:21:32.570
to study into to what extent
English and American are two

00:21:32.570 --> 00:21:35.990
different languages,
there are some usages

00:21:35.990 --> 00:21:39.380
which I recall were well-known.

00:21:39.380 --> 00:21:44.840
I suppose that,
according to studies

00:21:44.840 --> 00:21:46.820
which have been written
on this subject,

00:21:46.820 --> 00:21:50.570
British English says so long
as you're happy, we'll stay.

00:21:50.570 --> 00:21:53.960
American English, as long as
you are happy, we'll stay.

00:21:53.960 --> 00:21:57.350
Strange as it may seem in
British English, as strange

00:21:57.350 --> 00:21:59.630
as it may seem in American.

00:21:59.630 --> 00:22:03.440
Shall I take it in British,
should I take it in American.

00:22:03.440 --> 00:22:06.230
I wish I had done it in British.

00:22:06.230 --> 00:22:10.310
I wish I would have done it,
but probably not quite true.

00:22:10.310 --> 00:22:14.930
Or try other kinds of
vocabulary particularity,

00:22:14.930 --> 00:22:17.645
I think, to cater
for is British,

00:22:17.645 --> 00:22:20.300
and to cater to is American.

00:22:20.300 --> 00:22:23.000
So this is a lexical difference.

00:22:23.000 --> 00:22:26.270
Lorry is immediately identified
as British, as opposed

00:22:26.270 --> 00:22:29.450
to truck, which is
identified as American.

00:22:29.450 --> 00:22:34.000
Nappy and diaper, sitting
room and living room, dustbin

00:22:34.000 --> 00:22:39.230
and garbage can, chemist shop
and drugstore, and so on.

00:22:39.230 --> 00:22:44.860
But of course, all these are no
more than details and cannot--

00:22:44.860 --> 00:22:48.880
I also have blue movie for
pornographic film, nudie

00:22:48.880 --> 00:22:52.330
for film with much nudity.

00:22:52.330 --> 00:22:57.370
They cannot suffice to make up,
decide that we have to do with

00:22:57.370 --> 00:22:59.050
two different languages.

00:22:59.050 --> 00:23:06.310
So leaving the subject of
whether English and American

00:23:06.310 --> 00:23:09.250
are two different languages--
they are just two norms--

00:23:09.250 --> 00:23:13.330
I will switch to another one
which is more interesting.

00:23:13.330 --> 00:23:19.370
What are exactly the
kind of situations

00:23:19.370 --> 00:23:22.970
in which people
are when they are

00:23:22.970 --> 00:23:26.510
candidates to learning English?

00:23:26.510 --> 00:23:30.800
First, we can have to do
with an acquisitional aspect

00:23:30.800 --> 00:23:33.230
of the problem.

00:23:33.230 --> 00:23:35.810
In acquisitional
terms, we will speak

00:23:35.810 --> 00:23:39.640
our first language, second
language, and foreign language.

00:23:39.640 --> 00:23:41.910
But we have also
another possibility,

00:23:41.910 --> 00:23:45.290
which is to use a
sociocultural parameter.

00:23:45.290 --> 00:23:47.840
And in that respect,
we will speak

00:23:47.840 --> 00:23:52.280
of transplanted and
non-transplanted speakers

00:23:52.280 --> 00:23:53.480
of English.

00:23:53.480 --> 00:23:56.720
We also have a
motivational parameter

00:23:56.720 --> 00:24:00.710
according to which we can speak
of integrative and instrumental

00:24:00.710 --> 00:24:01.970
access to English.

00:24:01.970 --> 00:24:05.210
And finally, we have a
functional parameter,

00:24:05.210 --> 00:24:07.970
according to which we
have national language

00:24:07.970 --> 00:24:09.650
and international language.

00:24:09.650 --> 00:24:13.250
Such are the parameters
which I would

00:24:13.250 --> 00:24:21.480
propose as useful to decide
what we have to do with.

00:24:21.480 --> 00:24:25.620
Now the non-native
varieties of English.

00:24:25.620 --> 00:24:29.850
This is what I would
like to treat now.

00:24:29.850 --> 00:24:32.880
I announced that it is
not possible to speak

00:24:32.880 --> 00:24:34.320
any longer of English.

00:24:34.320 --> 00:24:37.320
What should we speak of?

00:24:37.320 --> 00:24:41.610
The term which has been proposed
more and more by sociolinguists

00:24:41.610 --> 00:24:45.300
is a pluralization of
the adjective English.

00:24:45.300 --> 00:24:48.530
They speak of Englishes,
and these Englishes

00:24:48.530 --> 00:24:51.460
are the various forms
that English takes,

00:24:51.460 --> 00:24:53.580
according to the country.

00:24:53.580 --> 00:24:55.920
The non-native
varieties of English--

00:24:55.920 --> 00:24:59.220
because Englishes refers to
these non-native varieties--

00:24:59.220 --> 00:25:02.460
they furnish fertile and
relatively untapped grounds

00:25:02.460 --> 00:25:06.330
for study of the processes
of sociolinguistic change.

00:25:06.330 --> 00:25:09.375
These varieties have
grown up in recent times

00:25:09.375 --> 00:25:12.300
as second languages in
multilingual former colonies

00:25:12.300 --> 00:25:13.350
of Great Britain.

00:25:13.350 --> 00:25:17.730
Hence the alternative terms
New Englishes, Third World

00:25:17.730 --> 00:25:21.360
Englishes, and also
NNVE, the famous NNVE,

00:25:21.360 --> 00:25:24.480
which means non-native
varieties of English.

00:25:24.480 --> 00:25:28.560
[INAUDIBLE] NNVE, it refers
to the Englishes used

00:25:28.560 --> 00:25:30.780
in many former
colonies of Britain

00:25:30.780 --> 00:25:33.740
or the United States
in which English is not

00:25:33.740 --> 00:25:38.610
a native language, but an
acquired second language.

00:25:38.610 --> 00:25:41.310
The last area to come
under colonization

00:25:41.310 --> 00:25:44.810
whether as British colonies or
as protectorates of Australia

00:25:44.810 --> 00:25:48.840
or New Zealand, were the South
Pacific Islands, Tonga, Samoa,

00:25:48.840 --> 00:25:52.740
Nauru, Fiji, Papua New
Guinea, Tuvalu, Ellis Islands,

00:25:52.740 --> 00:25:55.290
the Gilberts, and
the Solomon Islands,

00:25:55.290 --> 00:25:59.560
which were colonized in the late
19th and early 20th centuries.

00:25:59.560 --> 00:26:03.720
All are independent now, while
the Cook Islands, American

00:26:03.720 --> 00:26:07.370
Samoa, [INAUDIBLE],, and the
[INAUDIBLE] are not yet free.

00:26:07.370 --> 00:26:12.090
They are, let us say, without
polemicizing, territories

00:26:12.090 --> 00:26:14.860
under American influence.

00:26:14.860 --> 00:26:18.770
All these territories have
English as a second language.

00:26:18.770 --> 00:26:27.030
Now we can also speak of IVEs,
to wit, indigenized varieties

00:26:27.030 --> 00:26:28.020
of English.

00:26:28.020 --> 00:26:30.690
And these indigenized
varieties of English

00:26:30.690 --> 00:26:35.490
are more or less the
same as the NNVE.

00:26:35.490 --> 00:26:38.250
And they have been
neglected very long.

00:26:38.250 --> 00:26:42.960
And only recently have the
linguists and sociolinguists

00:26:42.960 --> 00:26:46.890
become aware of the importance
of studying these varieties.

00:26:46.890 --> 00:26:49.920
So what I would
like to do now is

00:26:49.920 --> 00:26:55.470
to propose a study of
some of these variants.

00:26:55.470 --> 00:27:00.960
Let me first of all mention
the Indian variants,

00:27:00.960 --> 00:27:03.380
which are among the
most important number.

00:27:07.710 --> 00:27:13.830
A wide range of styles can
be found in a small book.

00:27:13.830 --> 00:27:17.100
[INAUDIBLE],, the 19-year-old
illiterate sweeper

00:27:17.100 --> 00:27:20.427
who is the title character,
often seems quite childlike

00:27:20.427 --> 00:27:21.760
in thought as well as in speech.

00:27:21.760 --> 00:27:25.050
But he represents
what kind of usage

00:27:25.050 --> 00:27:29.850
many Indians, less educated
Indians, have of English.

00:27:29.850 --> 00:27:31.470
Occasionally the
reader encounters

00:27:31.470 --> 00:27:33.390
dialogues such as this.

00:27:33.390 --> 00:27:35.910
"My father is ill,"
replied [INAUDIBLE],,

00:27:35.910 --> 00:27:39.210
"so I am going to
sweep the roads in town

00:27:39.210 --> 00:27:42.570
and the temple
courtyard in his stead."

00:27:42.570 --> 00:27:47.370
So he uses ill, which
seems probably a bit odd

00:27:47.370 --> 00:27:49.680
to a native speaker, ill.

00:27:49.680 --> 00:27:53.910
And he says also, in his
stead instead of instead.

00:27:53.910 --> 00:27:57.885
He also used Indian words
such as [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH],,

00:27:57.885 --> 00:27:59.760
which means honor,
[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH] which

00:27:59.760 --> 00:28:02.390
means merchant caste,
[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH],,

00:28:02.390 --> 00:28:04.980
which is Indian sweets,
[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH],,

00:28:04.980 --> 00:28:07.437
which means England abroad.

00:28:07.437 --> 00:28:10.020
In fact, [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]
is an Arabic word which has been

00:28:10.020 --> 00:28:15.120
borrowed through Islamisation
by the Indians and which refers

00:28:15.120 --> 00:28:16.290
to a country.

00:28:16.290 --> 00:28:18.226
In this particular case
it refers to England.

00:28:18.226 --> 00:28:20.850
And also [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH],,
which is an Indian word meaning

00:28:20.850 --> 00:28:22.530
native or indigenous.

00:28:22.530 --> 00:28:25.260
This is a sample
of Indian English,

00:28:25.260 --> 00:28:29.790
but of course, I might
mention many other ones.

00:28:29.790 --> 00:28:32.550
Another one I take
from simply the titles

00:28:32.550 --> 00:28:40.510
of newspapers in which
almost 3/4 of the title

00:28:40.510 --> 00:28:43.000
are made of Indian
words which are not

00:28:43.000 --> 00:28:46.840
understandable to a native
English speaker of United

00:28:46.840 --> 00:28:48.310
Kingdom or United States.

00:28:48.310 --> 00:28:52.470
For example, in the rising
in Nepal in Kathmandu, 1978,

00:28:52.470 --> 00:28:55.990
"Panchayat system upholds
ideals of human rights."

00:28:55.990 --> 00:28:59.560
In the Hindustan Times,
[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH] are gas

00:28:59.560 --> 00:29:00.710
plants.

00:29:00.710 --> 00:29:03.940
In the Bangladesh Observer,
[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH] branch

00:29:03.940 --> 00:29:04.960
[INAUDIBLE].

00:29:04.960 --> 00:29:08.840
In [INAUDIBLE],, a journal
from Lahore, December 1979,

00:29:08.840 --> 00:29:11.980
"[INAUDIBLE] calls for attack."

00:29:11.980 --> 00:29:14.190
In the Sunday
Times of Singapore,

00:29:14.190 --> 00:29:17.110
Indian Muslims are
[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]..

00:29:17.110 --> 00:29:20.210
In the Statesman
of New Delhi, GNU,

00:29:20.210 --> 00:29:25.510
[INAUDIBLE] National University,
[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH],,

00:29:25.510 --> 00:29:26.060
and so on.

00:29:26.060 --> 00:29:28.300
Of course, this is
understandable only

00:29:28.300 --> 00:29:29.845
in an Indian context.

00:29:32.440 --> 00:29:36.490
A much more extreme
case is when syntax

00:29:36.490 --> 00:29:40.810
itself is Indian while
the vocabulary is English.

00:29:40.810 --> 00:29:46.980
For example, in an
example I have here,

00:29:46.980 --> 00:29:51.330
a linguist overheard a young
female Fiji Indian sales clerk.

00:29:51.330 --> 00:29:54.030
She said, "Sheila, account
book use [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH],,

00:29:54.030 --> 00:29:55.320
I think."

00:29:55.320 --> 00:29:58.770
Account book use is the reverse
order as opposed to that

00:29:58.770 --> 00:30:01.550
of English, and
[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH] is

00:30:01.550 --> 00:30:02.530
to make.

00:30:02.530 --> 00:30:04.440
So it means, in
fact, in translation,

00:30:04.440 --> 00:30:07.550
Sheila used the
account book, I think.

00:30:07.550 --> 00:30:10.320
And in order to translate
used, she uses the word

00:30:10.320 --> 00:30:12.900
[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH] to
make, and she put the object

00:30:12.900 --> 00:30:15.090
complement before
instead of after,

00:30:15.090 --> 00:30:17.010
as is the usage in English.

00:30:17.010 --> 00:30:21.240
This is typical of a new syntax,
a new world order, which,

00:30:21.240 --> 00:30:25.230
of course, is totally
un-understandable in English

00:30:25.230 --> 00:30:26.910
of native speakers.

00:30:26.910 --> 00:30:36.290
Now let me give you
some other examples.

00:30:36.290 --> 00:30:42.190
But before I switch
to the examples--

00:30:42.190 --> 00:30:46.990
well, another one is the
example of Malaysian English,

00:30:46.990 --> 00:30:51.130
in which we find such
usages as this one.

00:30:51.130 --> 00:30:55.510
In an article in a newspaper,
"Equally certainly,

00:30:55.510 --> 00:30:57.970
25 authors and
two editors do not

00:30:57.970 --> 00:31:00.010
know enough to write this book.

00:31:00.010 --> 00:31:03.670
And by virtue of
knowledges and viewpoints,

00:31:03.670 --> 00:31:07.420
they may not provide as cohesive
a book as a single author."

00:31:07.420 --> 00:31:09.490
Another example,
"Parents' eagerness

00:31:09.490 --> 00:31:11.770
to teach their
six-month-old children

00:31:11.770 --> 00:31:14.110
the pre-linguistic
routine, bye bye,

00:31:14.110 --> 00:31:16.810
is one evidence of
their desire to show

00:31:16.810 --> 00:31:19.840
that their baby is on its way
to being a socialized person."

00:31:19.840 --> 00:31:22.660
I think knowledges in the
plural is un-American.

00:31:22.660 --> 00:31:24.880
And I think one
evidence with one

00:31:24.880 --> 00:31:28.480
before the mass abstract noun
evidence is also un-American.

00:31:28.480 --> 00:31:31.300
To some extent, this
usage is in Malaysia,

00:31:31.300 --> 00:31:33.460
a part of the world in
which British English was

00:31:33.460 --> 00:31:35.470
more influential than
American English,

00:31:35.470 --> 00:31:39.700
might be borrowed from
British usage, in which it

00:31:39.700 --> 00:31:44.200
is possible to pluralize
abstract or mass nouns,

00:31:44.200 --> 00:31:46.090
since, for example,
in British journals,

00:31:46.090 --> 00:31:49.570
we find things which would
probably be frowned upon

00:31:49.570 --> 00:31:53.380
in American English, such as
intimidations in the plural,

00:31:53.380 --> 00:32:00.130
or other abstract word nouns
used in the plural, too.

00:32:00.130 --> 00:32:03.610
These examples
are mentioned here

00:32:03.610 --> 00:32:09.010
in order to show what kind
of Asianization of English

00:32:09.010 --> 00:32:10.810
takes place in Malaysia.

00:32:10.810 --> 00:32:15.520
Now let me mention examples
of Africanization of English.

00:32:15.520 --> 00:32:19.390
This is taken from a letter,
a personal letter to which I

00:32:19.390 --> 00:32:22.340
had access some years ago.

00:32:22.340 --> 00:32:25.870
An inhabitant of Nigeria
writes to his relatives,

00:32:25.870 --> 00:32:30.400
"With much pleasure and respect,
I inscribe you this few lines--

00:32:30.400 --> 00:32:33.280
this few lines-- and with the
hope that it will meet you

00:32:33.280 --> 00:32:35.050
in good condition of health."

00:32:35.050 --> 00:32:37.030
This seems to be
literally translated from

00:32:37.030 --> 00:32:40.720
[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH],, the
language of this Nigerian

00:32:40.720 --> 00:32:42.790
child.

00:32:42.790 --> 00:32:49.510
Other examples taken from people
living in Kenya and Tanzania.

00:32:49.510 --> 00:32:52.540
"Let strong football
team be organized."

00:32:52.540 --> 00:32:55.870
"He won by overwhelming
majority," without the article.

00:32:55.870 --> 00:32:58.100
"She gave me tough time."

00:32:58.100 --> 00:32:59.930
"I am going to cinema."

00:32:59.930 --> 00:33:01.560
"I am going to post office."

00:33:01.560 --> 00:33:06.044
Of course, these speakers
have as a native tongue

00:33:06.044 --> 00:33:08.460
a language which doesn't have
any article in this context.

00:33:08.460 --> 00:33:13.390
So they simply transfer their
habits into their English.

00:33:13.390 --> 00:33:15.250
"I may continue
with the interview

00:33:15.250 --> 00:33:18.450
or examine few
more applications."

00:33:18.450 --> 00:33:21.910
Or many other.

00:33:21.910 --> 00:33:26.720
Now another, which is taken
from the southeast of Nigeria,

00:33:26.720 --> 00:33:29.880
where [? Igbo, ?] a very
well-known African language,

00:33:29.880 --> 00:33:33.430
is spoken, in which
I overheard this.

00:33:33.430 --> 00:33:36.970
"'You'll see red,' said
the angry carpenter

00:33:36.970 --> 00:33:39.000
to the frightened boy."

00:33:39.000 --> 00:33:39.720
Another example.

00:33:39.720 --> 00:33:42.770
"To my surprise, I found
him, the driver, resting

00:33:42.770 --> 00:33:45.690
on the steer and fall asleep.

00:33:45.690 --> 00:33:48.810
As a result, he lost
control of the steer."

00:33:48.810 --> 00:33:49.890
Another sentence.

00:33:49.890 --> 00:33:53.700
"I ask her to dance,
but she cut me."

00:33:53.700 --> 00:33:54.730
Another example.

00:33:54.730 --> 00:33:57.090
"Thy must pay the town council."

00:33:57.090 --> 00:34:00.450
So these usages are taken
from African languages,

00:34:00.450 --> 00:34:02.280
from Igbo in this
particular case.

00:34:02.280 --> 00:34:07.170
To see red means it's a threats
to harm or punish a person.

00:34:07.170 --> 00:34:09.570
The steer here means
a steering wheel,

00:34:09.570 --> 00:34:13.679
and the verb to cut in the
sentence on I ask her to dance,

00:34:13.679 --> 00:34:18.150
but she cut me means she
refused to dance with me.

00:34:18.150 --> 00:34:21.840
So this is another
examples of the way English

00:34:21.840 --> 00:34:24.389
is Africanized in this context.

00:34:24.389 --> 00:34:26.480
Other examples still.

00:34:26.480 --> 00:34:29.340
"You to be careful with
these been to boys."

00:34:29.340 --> 00:34:30.989
What does been to mean?

00:34:30.989 --> 00:34:36.600
Been to is a special African
expression which is taken

00:34:36.600 --> 00:34:38.770
from she has been to Britain.

00:34:38.770 --> 00:34:42.690
And it refers to anyone who has
traveled overseas, particularly

00:34:42.690 --> 00:34:44.010
to Britain.

00:34:44.010 --> 00:34:45.210
Another example.

00:34:45.210 --> 00:34:47.070
"We stopped at
[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH] to buy

00:34:47.070 --> 00:34:50.850
some bush meat, which
means game, in fact,

00:34:50.850 --> 00:34:53.790
in native English.

00:34:53.790 --> 00:34:57.450
And "I saw your my dear.

00:34:57.450 --> 00:34:59.520
I saw your my dear
at the church.

00:34:59.520 --> 00:35:01.860
Your my dear [INAUDIBLE]
means your girlfriends

00:35:01.860 --> 00:35:05.280
or your boyfriends, my dear
being put together in order

00:35:05.280 --> 00:35:09.810
to constitute an expression
which corresponds to girl

00:35:09.810 --> 00:35:11.640
or boyfriend in English.

00:35:11.640 --> 00:35:13.620
"Ia have been going to
the small room a lot,

00:35:13.620 --> 00:35:16.440
sir" means to the toilets.

00:35:16.440 --> 00:35:19.590
And "I was a tight
friend of your sister."

00:35:19.590 --> 00:35:22.950
Tight friend, of
course, does not

00:35:22.950 --> 00:35:25.860
correspond to anything clear
for a native speaker of English,

00:35:25.860 --> 00:35:28.920
and it means a close
or intimate friend.

00:35:28.920 --> 00:35:34.410
It's almost literally translated
from African languages.

00:35:34.410 --> 00:35:38.040
I could multiply the languages,
but I will limit myself

00:35:38.040 --> 00:35:41.790
to some more only.

00:35:41.790 --> 00:35:47.430
[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH] Swahili
in Republic of Congo also have

00:35:47.430 --> 00:35:50.340
many uses which do not
correspond to the English

00:35:50.340 --> 00:35:51.510
American norm.

00:35:51.510 --> 00:35:54.170
For example, "Don't
drag your feet, son.

00:35:54.170 --> 00:35:56.400
Walk quick, quick."

00:35:56.400 --> 00:35:59.640
The reduplication of
quick means very quickly.

00:35:59.640 --> 00:36:00.840
"Life is a big challenge.

00:36:00.840 --> 00:36:03.820
You have to take
it small small."

00:36:03.820 --> 00:36:05.430
Or "You are eating too fastly.

00:36:05.430 --> 00:36:08.650
Take your time and
eat slow slow."

00:36:08.650 --> 00:36:12.270
This way of
reduplicating adverbs

00:36:12.270 --> 00:36:14.670
is simply the formation
in many African languages

00:36:14.670 --> 00:36:18.130
of the superlative,
or intensive,

00:36:18.130 --> 00:36:21.570
which is a grammatical process,
of course, unknown in English,

00:36:21.570 --> 00:36:23.940
although it's not quite
unknown in spoken English.

00:36:23.940 --> 00:36:27.950
But it does not correspond to
the written norm of English.

00:36:27.950 --> 00:36:28.575
Other examples.

00:36:32.430 --> 00:36:35.360
"He's my bench
man," meaning he's

00:36:35.360 --> 00:36:37.760
my crony or intimate friend.

00:36:37.760 --> 00:36:42.500
"She bluffs too much," bluff
meaning here, being arrogant

00:36:42.500 --> 00:36:48.170
or trying to beguile people.

00:36:48.170 --> 00:36:51.680
Or "The fellow is too
boisterous too much,"

00:36:51.680 --> 00:36:53.400
the storekeeper said.

00:36:53.400 --> 00:36:58.500
Here boisterous mean bad
tempered or quarrelsome.

00:36:58.500 --> 00:37:01.510
Other examples.

00:37:01.510 --> 00:37:03.710
"I was coupled at the dance."

00:37:03.710 --> 00:37:06.400
It means I found
a dancing partner.

00:37:06.400 --> 00:37:09.650
Or "Are you [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]
for Nigeria yet?"

00:37:09.650 --> 00:37:12.430
Are you homesick, it means.

00:37:12.430 --> 00:37:16.521
Or "I opened the door and
visualized a very familiar

00:37:16.521 --> 00:37:17.020
face."

00:37:17.020 --> 00:37:22.090
It means I saw, to
visualize a familiar face.

00:37:22.090 --> 00:37:25.280
"Sorry not to have been
chance to ride before."

00:37:25.280 --> 00:37:33.340
This is also an Africanism, and
many other of the same type.

00:37:33.340 --> 00:37:35.560
Voice out for voice.

00:37:35.560 --> 00:37:37.480
Discuss about for discuss.

00:37:37.480 --> 00:37:40.930
Congratulate for
for congratulate on.

00:37:40.930 --> 00:37:43.270
And many others.

00:37:43.270 --> 00:37:50.380
An anecdote, of which I was
myself an actor, a testimony,

00:37:50.380 --> 00:37:54.430
is one when one important
personality in Nigeria,

00:37:54.430 --> 00:37:56.680
while visiting a
department of linguistics,

00:37:56.680 --> 00:37:59.770
was told that this department
was interested in research

00:37:59.770 --> 00:38:01.600
on Nigerian English.

00:38:01.600 --> 00:38:05.290
He immediately commented
that that was a waste of time

00:38:05.290 --> 00:38:08.920
as there was no such thing
as Nigerian English, he said.

00:38:08.920 --> 00:38:12.400
But a few minutes later, he
said, on being interrupted,

00:38:12.400 --> 00:38:14.110
"Let me land."

00:38:14.110 --> 00:38:17.570
And to land is the Nigerian
equivalent of let me finish.

00:38:17.570 --> 00:38:24.130
So he himself, of course, spoke
a variant of Nigerian English.

00:38:24.130 --> 00:38:26.860
Now we also have
Chinese English.

00:38:26.860 --> 00:38:32.080
And it began as a
pidgin in Chinese

00:38:32.080 --> 00:38:34.849
as soon as the beginning
of the 19th century.

00:38:34.849 --> 00:38:36.640
And this Chinese English
was very different

00:38:36.640 --> 00:38:37.600
from standard English.

00:38:37.600 --> 00:38:41.030
For example, consider
the following sentence,

00:38:41.030 --> 00:38:46.030
which I tried to translate.

00:38:46.030 --> 00:38:50.620
"Tailor, my have got one
piece plenty handsome silk.

00:38:50.620 --> 00:38:54.360
My want you make one
nice evening dress."

00:38:54.360 --> 00:38:56.950
Here the my is used for me.

00:38:56.950 --> 00:39:02.650
And this kind of creolization,
in which the possessive

00:39:02.650 --> 00:39:04.660
is the same as the
personal pronoun,

00:39:04.660 --> 00:39:06.880
is typical of many Creoles.

00:39:06.880 --> 00:39:10.360
Although not
universally creole, it

00:39:10.360 --> 00:39:15.040
corresponds to a regularization
of the morphology.

00:39:15.040 --> 00:39:19.060
And it makes it much
more clear than it

00:39:19.060 --> 00:39:22.120
is in a language such
as English, in which we

00:39:22.120 --> 00:39:25.780
differentiate between word
classes, which are not really

00:39:25.780 --> 00:39:31.310
necessary in terms of economy.

00:39:31.310 --> 00:39:34.550
Another example taken
from China, from Chinese.

00:39:34.550 --> 00:39:39.010
In Chinese we have
examples like this one.

00:39:39.010 --> 00:39:42.320
Do some people
understand Chinese?

00:39:42.320 --> 00:39:46.300
Well, may I mention
in Chinese, [CHINESE]..

00:39:53.680 --> 00:39:58.450
This is a structure which is
directly inspired by English.

00:39:58.450 --> 00:40:01.630
It puts the object complement
in the wrong place,

00:40:01.630 --> 00:40:06.670
and it makes an inversion,
which is not Chinese.

00:40:06.670 --> 00:40:09.520
But all the words are
literally Chinese.

00:40:09.520 --> 00:40:17.590
So word order can be transformed
by the model of Chinese

00:40:17.590 --> 00:40:20.110
even when the words
themselves, the lexicon,

00:40:20.110 --> 00:40:22.750
remain something quite Chinese.

00:40:22.750 --> 00:40:28.090
This is another example of the
way languages are Americanized.

00:40:28.090 --> 00:40:31.060
Japanese is also an
interesting example.

00:40:31.060 --> 00:40:33.430
Mori Arinori.

00:40:33.430 --> 00:40:35.800
Who was the first
Minister of Education

00:40:35.800 --> 00:40:39.330
of the new modern
Japan, claimed that "I

00:40:39.330 --> 00:40:41.680
cite our meager
language, which can never

00:40:41.680 --> 00:40:44.230
be of any use outside
of our islands,

00:40:44.230 --> 00:40:46.060
is doomed to yield
to the domination

00:40:46.060 --> 00:40:47.890
of the English
tongue, especially

00:40:47.890 --> 00:40:50.710
when the power of
steam and electricity

00:40:50.710 --> 00:40:52.870
will have pervaded the land."

00:40:52.870 --> 00:40:58.750
He proposed to replace
Japanese, bluntly, by English.

00:40:58.750 --> 00:41:00.240
But the Japanese
did not do that.

00:41:00.240 --> 00:41:01.300
What did they do?

00:41:01.300 --> 00:41:04.100
They are probably the
one, among the countries

00:41:04.100 --> 00:41:07.030
of the world, in which the
borrowed word from English

00:41:07.030 --> 00:41:08.260
are the most numerous.

00:41:08.260 --> 00:41:11.140
It is extraordinary how many
borrowed term-- of course,

00:41:11.140 --> 00:41:17.530
Japanized with a the phonetic
exist, in nowadays Japanese.

00:41:17.530 --> 00:41:20.500
They are English, but not
identifiable, not recognizable

00:41:20.500 --> 00:41:22.750
to someone who does
not know their origin.

00:41:22.750 --> 00:41:26.210
For example, we have [JAPANESE].

00:41:26.210 --> 00:41:28.750
[JAPANESE],, which is playboy,
pronounced the Japanese way.

00:41:28.750 --> 00:41:31.180
[JAPANESE],, as you know, are
one and the same consonant

00:41:31.180 --> 00:41:32.500
in Japanese.

00:41:32.500 --> 00:41:35.430
And also, the Japanese
is a consonant vowel,

00:41:35.430 --> 00:41:37.250
consonant vowel,
consonant vowel language.

00:41:37.250 --> 00:41:38.650
So it cannot say playboy.

00:41:38.650 --> 00:41:42.100
It is transformed
into [JAPANESE]..

00:41:42.100 --> 00:41:46.010
And also magical power,
magical power for magic power.

00:41:46.010 --> 00:41:49.510
Also, [JAPANESE] for Dutch wife.

00:41:49.510 --> 00:41:51.850
Dutch wife is an
inflatable bed partner.

00:41:55.450 --> 00:42:02.150
Other examples of Japanese
English are [JAPANESE]..

00:42:02.150 --> 00:42:05.450
[JAPANESE] is love to make,
as you know, in Japanese.

00:42:05.450 --> 00:42:09.480
[JAPANESE] is a
well-known Japanese verb.

00:42:09.480 --> 00:42:14.140
And they take an English word,
which is, in this case, the up,

00:42:14.140 --> 00:42:17.020
preposition up, and they
put [JAPANESE] after,

00:42:17.020 --> 00:42:20.380
and [JAPANESE],, which
means it to make up,

00:42:20.380 --> 00:42:23.350
means, in fact, to
improve or go up.

00:42:23.350 --> 00:42:27.460
And we have many other such
uses in Japanese today.

00:42:27.460 --> 00:42:31.930
We also have [JAPANESE],,
Christmas, which is Christmas.

00:42:31.930 --> 00:42:34.780
We have [JAPANESE],,
[JAPANESE],, which is

00:42:34.780 --> 00:42:38.380
McDonald's, [JAPANESE],,
which is restaurant,

00:42:38.380 --> 00:42:42.770
and even [JAPANESE]
nonsense, [JAPANESE],,

00:42:42.770 --> 00:42:45.300
[? heretic ?]
grotesque nonsense.

00:42:45.300 --> 00:42:49.290
[JAPANESE],, which is ole
miss to refer to a spinster.

00:42:49.290 --> 00:42:52.070
[JAPANESE], mass communication.

00:42:52.070 --> 00:42:55.230
[JAPANESE] for one piece dress.

00:42:55.230 --> 00:42:59.560
[JAPANESE] for table
speech, after dinner speech.

00:42:59.560 --> 00:43:01.480
[JAPANESE] for at home.

00:43:01.480 --> 00:43:06.160
And even [JAPANESE],,
for I love you.

00:43:06.160 --> 00:43:13.060
These are some ways of Japanese
transformation of English

00:43:13.060 --> 00:43:16.170
or Japanization of English.

00:43:16.170 --> 00:43:25.220
Now this is all the
more astonishing

00:43:25.220 --> 00:43:29.750
as Japan was never a British
or American territory.

00:43:29.750 --> 00:43:32.480
Japan was never colonized by
the British, who elsewhere

00:43:32.480 --> 00:43:35.330
left a linguistic legacy in
the form of civil service

00:43:35.330 --> 00:43:38.630
requirements, government
legislation, or a prestige

00:43:38.630 --> 00:43:39.620
language.

00:43:39.620 --> 00:43:42.200
Such factors must
have encouraged people

00:43:42.200 --> 00:43:44.351
to become fluent in spoken
and written English.

00:43:44.351 --> 00:43:46.850
English was never the language
of the Japanese public school

00:43:46.850 --> 00:43:49.910
system, though there were
intellectual incentives

00:43:49.910 --> 00:43:52.760
to acquire at least
a reading knowledge.

00:43:52.760 --> 00:43:56.450
Most important, Japan has never
had the cultural and linguistic

00:43:56.450 --> 00:43:59.510
pluralism which made English
convenient as a lingua

00:43:59.510 --> 00:44:02.480
franca, at least, or a
national language at most.

00:44:02.480 --> 00:44:08.780
In short, English in Japan
never became institutionalized.

00:44:08.780 --> 00:44:14.810
However, that developed a kind
of Japanized English, of which

00:44:14.810 --> 00:44:17.000
I give you some samples.

00:44:17.000 --> 00:44:20.240
This also reaches
European languages.

00:44:20.240 --> 00:44:23.970
And since the
matter is enormous,

00:44:23.970 --> 00:44:28.760
I will content myself worth
mentioning German borrowing

00:44:28.760 --> 00:44:36.720
such as English words
which are directly

00:44:36.720 --> 00:44:41.160
introduced in the context of
an article written in German.

00:44:41.160 --> 00:44:47.980
We have, for example,
hi-fi, aftershave lotion.

00:44:47.980 --> 00:44:52.420
We have brain washing
for [GERMAN],, spearhead

00:44:52.420 --> 00:44:56.060
for [GERMAN],, climber
for [GERMAN] involved

00:44:56.060 --> 00:44:58.720
for [GERMAN],, and
other such examples.

00:45:01.300 --> 00:45:07.230
Let us, if you don't mind,
switch now to French.

00:45:07.230 --> 00:45:10.530
French has twice been a
world language itself,

00:45:10.530 --> 00:45:12.360
in the 12th century,
as you may know,

00:45:12.360 --> 00:45:16.180
and again from the 17th
to the 19th century.

00:45:16.180 --> 00:45:18.890
But twice it has also
been on the passive end

00:45:18.890 --> 00:45:20.070
of the process--

00:45:20.070 --> 00:45:22.530
in the 16th century, when
the giant of the Renaissance,

00:45:22.530 --> 00:45:26.310
Italy, covered the west
with Italianisms, and now

00:45:26.310 --> 00:45:28.920
in the 20th century,
when we have

00:45:28.920 --> 00:45:31.140
the impact of Americanisms.

00:45:31.140 --> 00:45:33.390
The Italian episode,
although different

00:45:33.390 --> 00:45:35.250
in certain ways
from the American,

00:45:35.250 --> 00:45:37.740
is of considerable
interest to us.

00:45:37.740 --> 00:45:39.870
Since it has
concluded, it allows

00:45:39.870 --> 00:45:42.660
us to evaluate from a
historical perspective

00:45:42.660 --> 00:45:45.000
those facets which,
in the present day

00:45:45.000 --> 00:45:47.700
case of American
English, are still

00:45:47.700 --> 00:45:50.010
in the midst of evolution.

00:45:50.010 --> 00:45:52.530
The impact of the
Italian Renaissance

00:45:52.530 --> 00:45:54.300
hurt the French pride.

00:45:54.300 --> 00:45:57.060
It hurt what humanistic
tradition labeled the Hercule

00:45:57.060 --> 00:45:59.460
Gallois, the Gallic Hercules.

00:45:59.460 --> 00:46:03.450
In states where citizens feel a
political cultural inferiority,

00:46:03.450 --> 00:46:05.790
language turns into
their foremost symbol

00:46:05.790 --> 00:46:10.290
of national defense, defense
phrasing-- thank you.

00:46:10.290 --> 00:46:13.990
Linguistic patriots consider
borrowing anti-patriotic.

00:46:13.990 --> 00:46:16.800
[INAUDIBLE],, the great
sociolinguist of the times,

00:46:16.800 --> 00:46:22.110
wrote in 1549 "The same natural
law which requires everyone

00:46:22.110 --> 00:46:24.480
to defend his
birthplace likewise

00:46:24.480 --> 00:46:28.120
obliges us to watch over
the dignity of our language.

00:46:28.120 --> 00:46:30.630
Similarly, the foreign
language turns into a symbol

00:46:30.630 --> 00:46:32.070
of the foreigner himself.

00:46:32.070 --> 00:46:37.650
Since he is disliked, his
language will be disliked."

00:46:37.650 --> 00:46:41.010
Now the battle of the
French intelligentsia

00:46:41.010 --> 00:46:44.430
against Americanisms
is not devolved

00:46:44.430 --> 00:46:47.790
from a certain conservative
political and cultural

00:46:47.790 --> 00:46:49.400
attitude.

00:46:49.400 --> 00:46:52.910
The anti-attitude symbolizes a
belief in tradition and norm,

00:46:52.910 --> 00:46:57.890
in elitism, in intellectual
skepticism toward technology.

00:46:57.890 --> 00:47:05.750
This is the opinion of many
English-speaking linguists.

00:47:05.750 --> 00:47:08.270
But so far, I agree.

00:47:08.270 --> 00:47:14.380
But one of them
adds, "Conservatism

00:47:14.380 --> 00:47:16.300
in France, linguistic
conservatism,

00:47:16.300 --> 00:47:20.350
symbolizes an anti-democratic
reaction against the women,

00:47:20.350 --> 00:47:22.330
the young, and mass culture.

00:47:22.330 --> 00:47:25.510
It symbolizes anti-Americanism
and a rejection

00:47:25.510 --> 00:47:28.180
of multilingualism
and multiculturalism.

00:47:28.180 --> 00:47:29.800
The attitude against
Americanisms,

00:47:29.800 --> 00:47:33.460
in short, reflects the struggle
against the cultural and social

00:47:33.460 --> 00:47:35.800
revolution which
marks the decline

00:47:35.800 --> 00:47:37.760
of the traditional
French civilization.

00:47:37.760 --> 00:47:43.330
This is what [INAUDIBLE],, an
American sociolinguist, writes.

00:47:43.330 --> 00:47:44.980
I must say I don't agree.

00:47:44.980 --> 00:47:45.790
Why?

00:47:45.790 --> 00:47:49.690
Because, in fact, the
way many linguists

00:47:49.690 --> 00:47:55.120
and many intellectual writers
and so on in France react

00:47:55.120 --> 00:47:59.530
against what they feel as an
invasion of American words,

00:47:59.530 --> 00:48:05.880
or as a pure and simple
substitution of English

00:48:05.880 --> 00:48:09.760
to French in many
contexts, is not

00:48:09.760 --> 00:48:14.680
a reaction which is
limited to right milieus.

00:48:14.680 --> 00:48:18.100
Leftists are also concerned
that this attitude.

00:48:18.100 --> 00:48:23.960
In general, what we aim
at when defending French

00:48:23.960 --> 00:48:24.812
and when trying--

00:48:24.812 --> 00:48:26.520
not only in a defensive
attitude but also

00:48:26.520 --> 00:48:28.270
in an offensive
one-- to promote it

00:48:28.270 --> 00:48:32.110
is to propose other
models because we

00:48:32.110 --> 00:48:35.140
feel that in many
cases, the domination

00:48:35.140 --> 00:48:37.150
of the world by one
and the same language

00:48:37.150 --> 00:48:40.300
is not something to be
recommended because it results

00:48:40.300 --> 00:48:46.060
in a world in which
we have nothing

00:48:46.060 --> 00:48:51.670
to be [INAUDIBLE] about, just
one language, one culture.

00:48:51.670 --> 00:48:56.350
Of course, this is just an
idealized possible future which

00:48:56.350 --> 00:48:57.640
will not, I think, take place.

00:48:57.640 --> 00:49:00.010
But it is the
thing against which

00:49:00.010 --> 00:49:03.430
people who want to
preserve multiculturalism,

00:49:03.430 --> 00:49:05.200
multilingualism, fight.

00:49:05.200 --> 00:49:08.110
And I think that to that
extent, they are not wrong.

00:49:08.110 --> 00:49:10.810
So I will come
back to this later.

00:49:10.810 --> 00:49:16.690
It was just a
reflection on the way

00:49:16.690 --> 00:49:18.460
part of the French
cultural world

00:49:18.460 --> 00:49:23.550
reacts to the
pressure of English.

00:49:23.550 --> 00:49:26.520
Throughout the non-English
mother tongue world,

00:49:26.520 --> 00:49:30.060
English is currently associated
with practical and powerful

00:49:30.060 --> 00:49:31.440
pursuits.

00:49:31.440 --> 00:49:34.680
This becomes evident
with the image of French,

00:49:34.680 --> 00:49:38.460
and on the other hand, with that
of local integrative languages

00:49:38.460 --> 00:49:41.670
that have been fairly
recently standardized.

00:49:41.670 --> 00:49:45.030
Relative to the latter, English
is viewed as less suitable

00:49:45.030 --> 00:49:46.890
for military operations.

00:49:46.890 --> 00:49:49.710
Local soldiers do not know
that much English yet.

00:49:49.710 --> 00:49:54.340
For lying, joking,
cursing, or bargaining--

00:49:54.340 --> 00:49:57.370
for bargaining, English
is not adapted at all--

00:49:57.370 --> 00:49:59.550
and for unremediated prayer.

00:49:59.550 --> 00:50:01.380
On the other hand,
English is currently

00:50:01.380 --> 00:50:04.260
viewed as more suitable than
local integrative languages

00:50:04.260 --> 00:50:08.040
for science, international
diplomacy, industry, commerce,

00:50:08.040 --> 00:50:11.430
high oratory, and
pop songs, of course.

00:50:11.430 --> 00:50:14.220
Now the image of
English vis a vis French

00:50:14.220 --> 00:50:17.250
is even more revealing in
that the former has risen

00:50:17.250 --> 00:50:21.300
and the latter has begun to fall
in the international balance

00:50:21.300 --> 00:50:22.500
of power.

00:50:22.500 --> 00:50:24.660
In the third world,
excluding former anglophone

00:50:24.660 --> 00:50:27.060
and francophone
colonies, French is

00:50:27.060 --> 00:50:31.150
considered more suitable than
English for only one function.

00:50:31.150 --> 00:50:36.600
This is what Joshua Fishman,
a well-known sociolinguist

00:50:36.600 --> 00:50:40.470
of Yeshiva University writes.

00:50:40.470 --> 00:50:44.242
I don't agree, but I
mention what he says.

00:50:44.242 --> 00:50:46.200
French is considered more
suitable than English

00:50:46.200 --> 00:50:49.309
for only one function,
which is opera.

00:50:49.309 --> 00:50:50.850
It is considered
the equal of English

00:50:50.850 --> 00:50:53.160
for reading good
novels or poetry,

00:50:53.160 --> 00:50:56.050
and for personal prayer, the
local integrity of language

00:50:56.050 --> 00:50:59.340
being widely viewed as superior
to both English and French

00:50:59.340 --> 00:51:01.140
in this connection,
which is obvious.

00:51:01.140 --> 00:51:05.890
But outside the realm of
aesthetics, the ugly duckling,

00:51:05.890 --> 00:51:08.880
Joshua Fishman writes,
reigns supreme.

00:51:08.880 --> 00:51:14.490
French is widely viewed as
more beautiful, more musical,

00:51:14.490 --> 00:51:18.390
pleasant, rhythmic, refined,
intimate, pure, soothing,

00:51:18.390 --> 00:51:21.120
graceful, tender, and lovely.

00:51:21.120 --> 00:51:24.910
But English is viewed
as richer, more precise,

00:51:24.910 --> 00:51:28.590
more logical more sophisticated,
and more competence-related.

00:51:28.590 --> 00:51:32.970
I must say this is a
widespread prejudice which,

00:51:32.970 --> 00:51:37.770
to me, is on the brink of
being quite ridiculous.

00:51:37.770 --> 00:51:41.460
Even under the pen of
Joshua Fishman, who

00:51:41.460 --> 00:51:44.610
is a well-known,
prestigious linguist,

00:51:44.610 --> 00:51:46.800
I think this should
not be written.

00:51:46.800 --> 00:51:50.610
Let me just tackle one
aspect of this subject, which

00:51:50.610 --> 00:51:53.970
is precision.

00:51:53.970 --> 00:51:57.540
You may know that professional
linguists like me generally

00:51:57.540 --> 00:52:03.810
do not commit themselves in
debates such as to what extent

00:52:03.810 --> 00:52:06.180
is one language more
precise than the other one

00:52:06.180 --> 00:52:08.820
because the notion of
precision is a relative notion.

00:52:08.820 --> 00:52:11.340
And for me, for any--

00:52:11.340 --> 00:52:13.130
I suppose for any
professional language,

00:52:13.130 --> 00:52:15.240
the most precise
language is the one

00:52:15.240 --> 00:52:17.220
which is native to
you, to each of you.

00:52:17.220 --> 00:52:20.580
Of course, there is
no precision in itself

00:52:20.580 --> 00:52:22.330
as an abstract concept.

00:52:22.330 --> 00:52:26.850
However, I would like to
adduce an example, which

00:52:26.850 --> 00:52:29.040
I have recently
discovered, which

00:52:29.040 --> 00:52:31.320
might be interesting
if I proposed it

00:52:31.320 --> 00:52:33.060
to you to your thinking.

00:52:33.060 --> 00:52:38.310
And in that case, it seems that
in a very politicized context,

00:52:38.310 --> 00:52:44.250
French is reacted to as much
more precise than English.

00:52:44.250 --> 00:52:50.340
This refers to Resolution 242
of the new United Nations.

00:52:50.340 --> 00:52:56.400
You may know that the
Arabian countries have chosen

00:52:56.400 --> 00:52:59.130
the French version
of the treaties,

00:52:59.130 --> 00:53:03.750
and Israel has chosen the
British, the English version.

00:53:03.750 --> 00:53:05.580
Why?

00:53:05.580 --> 00:53:10.350
The British-- the English
version says, we recommend.

00:53:10.350 --> 00:53:14.370
Probably they said, we
order withdrawal immediate--

00:53:14.370 --> 00:53:21.030
it was in 1968 after
the famous Six Days War.

00:53:21.030 --> 00:53:26.010
"We recommend withdrawal
of Israeli armed forces

00:53:26.010 --> 00:53:28.570
from occupied territories."

00:53:28.570 --> 00:53:30.930
This is the English version.

00:53:30.930 --> 00:53:36.630
In English, when you have a
plural term like territories,

00:53:36.630 --> 00:53:38.430
you may not use the article.

00:53:38.430 --> 00:53:41.880
So "from occupied territories"
is a normal English way

00:53:41.880 --> 00:53:42.720
of saying.

00:53:42.720 --> 00:53:45.870
And "from the occupied
territories" could be said.

00:53:45.870 --> 00:53:47.220
But in this particular case--

00:53:47.220 --> 00:53:48.761
I am not a native
speaker of English,

00:53:48.761 --> 00:53:52.380
I don't know-- but it seems to
me that this lack of article

00:53:52.380 --> 00:53:54.430
is the more natural
way of speaking.

00:53:54.430 --> 00:53:59.070
So withdrawal of
Israeli armed forces

00:53:59.070 --> 00:54:01.230
from occupied territories.

00:54:01.230 --> 00:54:04.770
In French, as opposed to
that, we cannot avoid using

00:54:04.770 --> 00:54:05.270
the article.

00:54:05.270 --> 00:54:09.550
We must choose either the
article or no article.

00:54:09.550 --> 00:54:11.910
So this a retrait--

00:54:11.910 --> 00:54:14.600
retrait, withdrawal,
to retract--

00:54:14.600 --> 00:54:19.210
des troupes Israelienne--
of Israeli armed troops--

00:54:19.210 --> 00:54:20.800
des territoires occupes.

00:54:20.800 --> 00:54:24.580
De means from the, or
de territoire occupe.

00:54:24.580 --> 00:54:28.510
If I say des territories
occupes, which is a choice,

00:54:28.510 --> 00:54:30.340
it means of all the
territories which have

00:54:30.340 --> 00:54:32.020
been occupied by the Israelis.

00:54:32.020 --> 00:54:34.540
If I say de
territoire occupe, it

00:54:34.540 --> 00:54:37.180
means of some of
these territories.

00:54:37.180 --> 00:54:38.690
And in French we have to choose.

00:54:38.690 --> 00:54:45.940
So the French version,
which is the one recommended

00:54:45.940 --> 00:54:51.280
by the Arabs, has chosen
des territoires occupes,

00:54:51.280 --> 00:54:54.460
which means that
Israel has to withdraw

00:54:54.460 --> 00:54:58.030
his troops from all
occupied territories,

00:54:58.030 --> 00:55:01.870
whereas English allows one to
interpret in ambiguous terms

00:55:01.870 --> 00:55:04.990
either as from some
of these territories

00:55:04.990 --> 00:55:07.210
or from all of them.

00:55:07.210 --> 00:55:08.420
This is an example.

00:55:08.420 --> 00:55:10.120
We might mention
many other ones,

00:55:10.120 --> 00:55:13.005
we met also, because, of course,
this is very relative matter--

00:55:13.005 --> 00:55:15.630
mention examples in which it is
English, which is more precise.

00:55:15.630 --> 00:55:18.920
It depends on the
kind of circumstance.

00:55:18.920 --> 00:55:21.940
French was-- used to be in
the past, is no more now--

00:55:21.940 --> 00:55:23.510
the language of diplomacy.

00:55:23.510 --> 00:55:27.610
I do not, as a
professional linguist,

00:55:27.610 --> 00:55:31.510
make part of those who
purport that French

00:55:31.510 --> 00:55:35.440
is a clear, precise language.

00:55:35.440 --> 00:55:38.290
As I said, to each
of us, the clearest

00:55:38.290 --> 00:55:39.940
is the one we master most.

00:55:39.940 --> 00:55:42.400
But what I can
call your attention

00:55:42.400 --> 00:55:45.970
to is that in certain
contexts, like this one, which

00:55:45.970 --> 00:55:48.970
is a very a politicized,
very political context,

00:55:48.970 --> 00:55:52.210
the French version can turn
out to be the more precise one.

00:55:52.210 --> 00:56:00.850
And so what Fishman writes
here is, to some extent, wrong.

00:56:00.850 --> 00:56:04.930
He adds, "English is
less loved but more used.

00:56:04.930 --> 00:56:08.020
French is more
loved but less used.

00:56:08.020 --> 00:56:12.820
Nature abhors a vacuum, which
is a medieval expression,

00:56:12.820 --> 00:56:13.570
well-known.

00:56:13.570 --> 00:56:16.150
As the functional load of a
formerly prestigious variety

00:56:16.150 --> 00:56:20.110
declines, affect rushes
in to take up the slack.

00:56:20.110 --> 00:56:22.570
The non-Francophone
world has nothing

00:56:22.570 --> 00:56:26.680
but love for French," he says,
"and in the cruel real world,

00:56:26.680 --> 00:56:28.630
that is a sign of a weakness.

00:56:28.630 --> 00:56:32.230
The displacement of Irish
is suffused with love.

00:56:32.230 --> 00:56:33.820
The replacement
of Yiddish, which

00:56:33.820 --> 00:56:38.020
is a well-known matter
for Joshua Fishman,

00:56:38.020 --> 00:56:41.140
is accompanied by panagyrics
as to its intimacy

00:56:41.140 --> 00:56:42.670
and authenticity.

00:56:42.670 --> 00:56:44.980
English gets along without
love, without sighs,

00:56:44.980 --> 00:56:47.950
without tears, and almost
without affect of any kind.

00:56:47.950 --> 00:56:49.250
This is not true.

00:56:49.250 --> 00:56:51.310
There is an affective
access to English

00:56:51.310 --> 00:56:57.370
for many speakers
of it, which are not

00:56:57.370 --> 00:56:59.860
native speakers of English,
but we have acquired it.

00:56:59.860 --> 00:57:04.860
So I don't agree that English
is something just as unmarked

00:57:04.860 --> 00:57:07.270
as Fishman writes.

00:57:07.270 --> 00:57:10.150
English gets along without love,
without sighs, without tears,

00:57:10.150 --> 00:57:12.100
almost without
affect of any kind.

00:57:12.100 --> 00:57:15.100
In a world where
econo-technical superiority

00:57:15.100 --> 00:57:18.250
is what really counts, the
heightened aesthetic affective

00:57:18.250 --> 00:57:21.280
image of French smacks
of weakness, innocence,

00:57:21.280 --> 00:57:22.690
and triviality.

00:57:22.690 --> 00:57:27.280
This is the opinion voiced by
a well-known social linguist.

00:57:27.280 --> 00:57:32.320
I am not sure that many
linguists outside myself

00:57:32.320 --> 00:57:33.910
would agree with it.

00:57:33.910 --> 00:57:38.320
Now this situation which
I have tried to describe,

00:57:38.320 --> 00:57:41.590
according to which
English is under the form

00:57:41.590 --> 00:57:44.020
of native English, in one case--

00:57:44.020 --> 00:57:45.310
a minority, of course--

00:57:45.310 --> 00:57:49.750
and in another case, in the
form of non-native varieties

00:57:49.750 --> 00:57:51.670
or indigenized
varieties of English,

00:57:51.670 --> 00:57:54.410
or of Englishes in the plural--

00:57:54.410 --> 00:58:02.680
this general situation
is viewed or reacted

00:58:02.680 --> 00:58:06.130
to by a great part
of intellectuals

00:58:06.130 --> 00:58:10.000
all over the world as something
not completely acceptable.

00:58:10.000 --> 00:58:10.840
Why?

00:58:10.840 --> 00:58:18.130
Because they sense, they feel
that having a language which

00:58:18.130 --> 00:58:21.912
serves the role of being the
second language for people

00:58:21.912 --> 00:58:23.620
whose language is not
known to each other

00:58:23.620 --> 00:58:27.130
when they are dialoguing is
not necessarily something good.

00:58:27.130 --> 00:58:31.520
When it turns out that this
language belongs to countries--

00:58:31.520 --> 00:58:34.630
Canada, United States,
Australia, New Zealand,

00:58:34.630 --> 00:58:37.720
South Africa-- which turn out
to be among the most powerful

00:58:37.720 --> 00:58:40.540
in the world, this makes
English much less acceptable

00:58:40.540 --> 00:58:42.100
than if it were not
the case that it

00:58:42.100 --> 00:58:43.570
belongs to these countries.

00:58:43.570 --> 00:58:47.270
This, of course, is to a large
extent a political matter.

00:58:47.270 --> 00:58:51.640
And as a result,
people not necessarily

00:58:51.640 --> 00:58:54.210
accept such a situation.

00:58:54.210 --> 00:58:59.140
And I would like to
mention a text which

00:58:59.140 --> 00:59:02.170
was submitted to me recently
by an American university

00:59:02.170 --> 00:59:02.920
professor.

00:59:02.920 --> 00:59:04.180
He is American.

00:59:04.180 --> 00:59:06.760
The text will
sounds very strange

00:59:06.760 --> 00:59:08.050
coming from an American.

00:59:08.050 --> 00:59:11.530
But what he says might be
interesting to mention.

00:59:11.530 --> 00:59:16.120
And I received this on
internet one month ago.

00:59:16.120 --> 00:59:19.250
He says, "According to the
latest figures supplied

00:59:19.250 --> 00:59:24.020
by Global Reach, English
content of all internet messages

00:59:24.020 --> 00:59:27.770
worldwide dropped below 50%.

00:59:27.770 --> 00:59:30.350
It is clear that as
the net goes global,

00:59:30.350 --> 00:59:32.570
it also goes multilingual.

00:59:32.570 --> 00:59:34.850
The internet was
born in English,

00:59:34.850 --> 00:59:37.400
but it has become quite
obvious that those

00:59:37.400 --> 00:59:40.400
who attempted to promote it
through the use of English

00:59:40.400 --> 00:59:45.740
only slowed down its development
rather than accelerating it.

00:59:45.740 --> 00:59:49.220
Once again, we are
discovering that localization

00:59:49.220 --> 00:59:53.450
is the key for the international
dissemination of any tool,

00:59:53.450 --> 00:59:55.940
and more especially when
that tool is designed

00:59:55.940 --> 00:59:58.250
to facilitate communication.

00:59:58.250 --> 00:59:59.840
It is well-known
that anyone who is

00:59:59.840 --> 01:00:02.540
serious about pursuing
commercial endeavors

01:00:02.540 --> 01:00:05.600
has to use his
customers' language.

01:00:05.600 --> 01:00:08.090
This policy-- which is,
by the way, I add myself,

01:00:08.090 --> 01:00:10.190
that of Germany--

01:00:10.190 --> 01:00:13.010
to a lesser extent, much
lesser extent than the French,

01:00:13.010 --> 01:00:16.280
was especially pushed by
firms that sought expansion

01:00:16.280 --> 01:00:18.770
through the development
of international markets.

01:00:18.770 --> 01:00:22.340
In the old days, the success
of firms such as IBM,

01:00:22.340 --> 01:00:24.290
as far as the Americans
are concerned,

01:00:24.290 --> 01:00:26.330
rested mostly on this approach.

01:00:26.330 --> 01:00:30.140
IBM intelligently translated
all technical manuals,

01:00:30.140 --> 01:00:33.620
offered seminars and training
in over 20 languages.

01:00:33.620 --> 01:00:37.490
IBM went as far as
translating pushbutton levels

01:00:37.490 --> 01:00:41.750
on its hardware and even
coining new [INAUDIBLE] words.

01:00:41.750 --> 01:00:45.170
That was the case, for instance,
with ordinateur, which is now

01:00:45.170 --> 01:00:47.210
the French word for computer.

01:00:47.210 --> 01:00:50.210
I must intervene
this is quite wrong.

01:00:50.210 --> 01:00:52.439
Ordinateur was not
invented by IBM.

01:00:52.439 --> 01:00:53.980
It was invented by
a French Latinist,

01:00:53.980 --> 01:00:57.920
Latin professor in the
Sorbonne, Jean [? Perret, ?]

01:00:57.920 --> 01:01:01.820
who proposed it to the
AFNOR, Academie Francaise de

01:01:01.820 --> 01:01:06.200
Terminologie, and who said,
instead of computeur, which

01:01:06.200 --> 01:01:08.900
was beginning then to
introduce itself in French,

01:01:08.900 --> 01:01:15.320
we should use a word which shows
that the machine in question

01:01:15.320 --> 01:01:18.650
is able to ordinate in the
meaning of organize together

01:01:18.650 --> 01:01:23.400
many data, many
elements of information.

01:01:23.400 --> 01:01:25.910
And the best word for
that is ordinateur--

01:01:25.910 --> 01:01:28.310
which he was a Catholic,
and of course he

01:01:28.310 --> 01:01:30.470
thought of ordination,
which means the consecration

01:01:30.470 --> 01:01:31.560
of a priest.

01:01:31.560 --> 01:01:35.300
But he took it from the
ecclesiastical vocabulary.

01:01:35.300 --> 01:01:37.020
But ordinateur was a success.

01:01:37.020 --> 01:01:40.160
And we know, because this has
a date and there are documents,

01:01:40.160 --> 01:01:44.810
that ordinateur was proposed by
this French professor of Latin

01:01:44.810 --> 01:01:47.270
and not by IBM.

01:01:47.270 --> 01:01:50.540
But this does not stop
me from recognizing

01:01:50.540 --> 01:01:56.030
that IBM has proposed many
felicitous neologisms.

01:01:56.030 --> 01:01:59.690
The success of Microsoft,
this professor adds,

01:01:59.690 --> 01:02:03.080
mostly relied on
the same approach.

01:02:03.080 --> 01:02:07.580
As far back as 1995,
Microsoft had already

01:02:07.580 --> 01:02:11.190
60% of its market outside
English speaking countries.

01:02:11.190 --> 01:02:14.150
Again, few people
and analysts note

01:02:14.150 --> 01:02:16.520
that this tremendous
success rested

01:02:16.520 --> 01:02:19.460
less on the quantity
of Microsoft products

01:02:19.460 --> 01:02:21.410
than the capability
of the company

01:02:21.410 --> 01:02:24.380
to sell in its
customers' tongues.

01:02:24.380 --> 01:02:26.960
The internet is
supposed to facilitate

01:02:26.960 --> 01:02:30.770
international communication,
not to preclude it.

01:02:30.770 --> 01:02:33.710
Yet it is surprising to
find out that many internet

01:02:33.710 --> 01:02:38.060
users believe that restricting
expression to English only

01:02:38.060 --> 01:02:41.000
on the net is necessary
to bridge our differences

01:02:41.000 --> 01:02:45.170
and make it possible for us to
fully understand one another.

01:02:45.170 --> 01:02:48.800
Is English really
adequate in this context?

01:02:48.800 --> 01:02:53.300
English is the native tongue
to a bare 6% of the world

01:02:53.300 --> 01:02:54.450
population.

01:02:54.450 --> 01:02:58.190
And even though it is
widely studied, over 70%

01:02:58.190 --> 01:03:01.070
of the world population
has no knowledge of it.

01:03:01.070 --> 01:03:03.830
If 20% or so of the
world population

01:03:03.830 --> 01:03:06.620
has some knowledge of
English as a second language,

01:03:06.620 --> 01:03:11.450
those of us who travel a lot can
testify that fluency in English

01:03:11.450 --> 01:03:13.220
in non-English
speaking countries

01:03:13.220 --> 01:03:14.840
is just wishful thinking.

01:03:14.840 --> 01:03:16.760
It applies to me,
as you can see.

01:03:16.760 --> 01:03:19.700
If English may be understood
well enough to us for us

01:03:19.700 --> 01:03:23.930
to check into a
hotel, order a meal,

01:03:23.930 --> 01:03:26.120
or tell a cabbie
where to take us,

01:03:26.120 --> 01:03:29.690
it does not often allow us to go
much beyond addressing our most

01:03:29.690 --> 01:03:31.040
immediate needs.

01:03:31.040 --> 01:03:33.380
True, English has
been widely adopted

01:03:33.380 --> 01:03:35.420
as the international
language for science.

01:03:35.420 --> 01:03:38.840
But can those of us who attend
international conferences

01:03:38.840 --> 01:03:42.080
honestly tell us that foreigners
can make themselves understood

01:03:42.080 --> 01:03:44.600
in English as well as we can?

01:03:44.600 --> 01:03:47.540
Haven't we noticed that
apart from a few exceptions,

01:03:47.540 --> 01:03:49.970
even highly educated
professionals

01:03:49.970 --> 01:03:52.150
whose mother tongue
is not English

01:03:52.150 --> 01:03:54.917
have a much harder time
to address our questions,

01:03:54.917 --> 01:03:56.750
and more especially
when their work is being

01:03:56.750 --> 01:03:58.640
questioned and criticized?

01:03:58.640 --> 01:04:01.640
Are we blind to the
post-conference syndrome

01:04:01.640 --> 01:04:03.740
that affects most of
the participants who

01:04:03.740 --> 01:04:07.070
speak English as a second
language when they congregate

01:04:07.070 --> 01:04:09.230
and regroup as soon
as the plenary session

01:04:09.230 --> 01:04:14.390
is over to communicate freely
in their own native tongues?

01:04:14.390 --> 01:04:16.490
In the hard sciences
and in technology,

01:04:16.490 --> 01:04:19.510
when PowerPoint slides
and transparencies

01:04:19.510 --> 01:04:21.550
can compensate for
the lack of fluency

01:04:21.550 --> 01:04:24.430
to present an experimental
setup, a pilot

01:04:24.430 --> 01:04:27.850
plant, or a bunch of equations
to model physical phenomena,

01:04:27.850 --> 01:04:31.340
English does not seem to
be much of an impediment.

01:04:31.340 --> 01:04:35.530
But can we really expect a top
level scholar in psychology,

01:04:35.530 --> 01:04:38.920
in social science, in
history, or in literature

01:04:38.920 --> 01:04:43.180
be able to present his research
in a fully effective way

01:04:43.180 --> 01:04:45.910
and in a manner as
convincing and persuasive

01:04:45.910 --> 01:04:47.440
as if he was conducting--

01:04:47.440 --> 01:04:50.780
I would have written
as if he were--

01:04:50.780 --> 01:04:51.650
as if he were.

01:04:51.650 --> 01:04:54.770
But the American
write as if he was--

01:04:54.770 --> 01:05:00.080
as if he was conducing his
talk in his own native tongue.

01:05:00.080 --> 01:05:01.170
Of course not.

01:05:01.170 --> 01:05:03.650
The widely known
Jacques Derrida used

01:05:03.650 --> 01:05:07.250
to give his talks in English
when he traveled to the US

01:05:07.250 --> 01:05:09.170
until his American
audiences told

01:05:09.170 --> 01:05:11.860
him to switch back to French.

01:05:11.860 --> 01:05:13.100
Why?

01:05:13.100 --> 01:05:16.060
Even though they did not fully
master the French language,

01:05:16.060 --> 01:05:18.740
Derrida was far more
understandable to them

01:05:18.740 --> 01:05:21.140
in his own native
talk, and even more

01:05:21.140 --> 01:05:25.310
so as they were already
familiar with his work.

01:05:25.310 --> 01:05:27.740
Language is not
neutral, and translation

01:05:27.740 --> 01:05:29.830
implies switching over
to a different system

01:05:29.830 --> 01:05:30.832
of coordinates.

01:05:34.940 --> 01:05:37.800
By adopting English as it
means for real international

01:05:37.800 --> 01:05:40.050
communication, I
will necessarily

01:05:40.050 --> 01:05:43.110
have to adapt to the
English speaking psyche

01:05:43.110 --> 01:05:45.660
and use references that are
common to English speaking

01:05:45.660 --> 01:05:48.180
countries and Anglo
American culture,

01:05:48.180 --> 01:05:51.660
thereby losing, in the process,
the best of my message.

01:05:51.660 --> 01:05:54.540
As a minority, what right
do native English speakers

01:05:54.540 --> 01:05:57.840
have to foist English
upon a world majority?

01:05:57.840 --> 01:05:59.670
Can we ever expect
non-English speakers

01:05:59.670 --> 01:06:02.220
to master English
as well as those who

01:06:02.220 --> 01:06:03.670
have it as their native tongue?

01:06:12.480 --> 01:06:14.950
Most of the time, a basic
knowledge of German, French,

01:06:14.950 --> 01:06:17.760
and English was sufficient
in the olden days.

01:06:23.490 --> 01:06:26.305
Then, in the 19th
century, the free flow

01:06:26.305 --> 01:06:27.930
of scientific and
technical information

01:06:27.930 --> 01:06:30.360
went unhampered by
multilingualism.

01:06:30.360 --> 01:06:33.630
On the contrary, it seems
to have boosted creativity.

01:06:33.630 --> 01:06:35.820
Scientific creativity feeds
on language and language

01:06:35.820 --> 01:06:38.460
structures, as the linguist
Benjamin Lee Whorf has clearly

01:06:38.460 --> 01:06:39.510
shown.

01:06:39.510 --> 01:06:41.580
A scientist who gives
up his own native tongue

01:06:41.580 --> 01:06:44.910
to conduct his work can
never reach full potential,

01:06:44.910 --> 01:06:47.820
and often he will be limited
to technical contributions

01:06:47.820 --> 01:06:51.440
only, even though he is
able to do much more.

01:06:51.440 --> 01:06:53.340
The tremendous
variety of languages

01:06:53.340 --> 01:06:56.670
appears less awesome when we
delineate language families

01:06:56.670 --> 01:06:59.100
inside which the
acquisition of another idiom

01:06:59.100 --> 01:07:01.680
can be made
relatively effortless.

01:07:01.680 --> 01:07:04.080
100 years ago in
Europe, serious students

01:07:04.080 --> 01:07:06.570
studied two or three
foreign languages,

01:07:06.570 --> 01:07:12.030
not to become fluent and
interpreters, not because they

01:07:12.030 --> 01:07:14.640
were mesmerized by any
superior civilization,

01:07:14.640 --> 01:07:17.940
but to be able to understand
their neighbors, and mostly

01:07:17.940 --> 01:07:19.830
those who made
significant contribution

01:07:19.830 --> 01:07:23.010
to their professional fields.

01:07:23.010 --> 01:07:29.970
So he conclude by saying,
"International forums

01:07:29.970 --> 01:07:32.520
and discussion groups
should welcome contributions

01:07:32.520 --> 01:07:35.520
in all languages if their
participants were really

01:07:35.520 --> 01:07:39.090
sticking with the best and
most interesting contributions.

01:07:39.090 --> 01:07:42.660
Instead, the internet shows
today incredible mediocrity.

01:07:42.660 --> 01:07:45.300
While the average citizen
thinks the internet brings him

01:07:45.300 --> 01:07:48.060
the world, the serious
intellectual has returned

01:07:48.060 --> 01:07:50.970
to his book, lecture
halls, conferences,

01:07:50.970 --> 01:07:53.400
and head-to-head discussions
and roundtable"--

01:07:53.400 --> 01:07:55.770
I don't say that I
agree fully with this--

01:07:55.770 --> 01:07:59.010
"and roundtables that gather
real, thought-challenging

01:07:59.010 --> 01:08:00.870
professionals and researchers.

01:08:00.870 --> 01:08:03.480
If people want the
best from the internet,

01:08:03.480 --> 01:08:05.520
they have to invite
back the best

01:08:05.520 --> 01:08:09.300
by first realizing that
original thought automatically

01:08:09.300 --> 01:08:13.030
entail the use of original
modes of expression."

01:08:13.030 --> 01:08:18.180
This is the opinion voiced by
someone, an American professor,

01:08:18.180 --> 01:08:24.090
who is aware or conscious of the
dangers of this uniformization

01:08:24.090 --> 01:08:25.080
by English.

01:08:25.080 --> 01:08:31.170
What would I myself propose
as a conclusion in all that?

01:08:31.170 --> 01:08:35.100
I think English is, in
fact, not so much a threat

01:08:35.100 --> 01:08:37.170
as it is often said to be.

01:08:37.170 --> 01:08:38.310
Why?

01:08:38.310 --> 01:08:42.779
First, because although much
more English speakers than

01:08:42.779 --> 01:08:47.770
the ones to which it is native
exist all over the world,

01:08:47.770 --> 01:08:51.090
it is native to a small
minority of the world's.

01:08:51.090 --> 01:08:56.580
Second, because it gives rise
to new forms of languages,

01:08:56.580 --> 01:09:00.979
new Englishes, which might, by
progressive transformations,

01:09:00.979 --> 01:09:04.370
yield new languages between
which there would not

01:09:04.370 --> 01:09:05.359
be any communication.

01:09:05.359 --> 01:09:09.979
This is the story of Latin
yielding Portuguese, Spanish,

01:09:09.979 --> 01:09:13.520
Romanian, French, and Italian,
so-called Neo-Latin or romance

01:09:13.520 --> 01:09:14.180
languages.

01:09:14.180 --> 01:09:15.580
And this is the fate--

01:09:15.580 --> 01:09:17.990
the word fate is not
felicitous because it is not

01:09:17.990 --> 01:09:19.790
something bad.

01:09:19.790 --> 01:09:23.340
It is just the normal
result of evolution.

01:09:23.340 --> 01:09:26.300
And this kind of
transformation of languages,

01:09:26.300 --> 01:09:30.819
which come from the same trunk,
the same original source,

01:09:30.819 --> 01:09:36.290
is something which has always
happened in the world in space

01:09:36.290 --> 01:09:37.350
and in time.

01:09:37.350 --> 01:09:40.850
So it has begun to
happen to English,

01:09:40.850 --> 01:09:44.240
as it does happen to French
also in former French colonial

01:09:44.240 --> 01:09:48.260
territories in which there are
some forms of French to which I

01:09:48.260 --> 01:09:53.240
referred yesterday in
a class of one of you,

01:09:53.240 --> 01:09:59.210
which are getting more and
more different from French such

01:09:59.210 --> 01:10:00.960
as it is spoken in France.

01:10:00.960 --> 01:10:06.190
So this second reason not to
be anxious about the future

01:10:06.190 --> 01:10:07.700
is, for me, an important one.

01:10:07.700 --> 01:10:15.080
A third one is that
English is frowned

01:10:15.080 --> 01:10:18.180
upon by those who
fend for diversity,

01:10:18.180 --> 01:10:21.420
which means that many people--

01:10:21.420 --> 01:10:23.000
not only in the
so-called elites,

01:10:23.000 --> 01:10:26.060
but also in the average public--

01:10:26.060 --> 01:10:29.840
react to the danger of
uniformization, which

01:10:29.840 --> 01:10:38.130
is the result of a very
dangerous pretext often

01:10:38.130 --> 01:10:41.190
provided by people who
argue in favor of English,

01:10:41.190 --> 01:10:43.380
saying that when we
have just one language,

01:10:43.380 --> 01:10:46.710
this allows us to communicate,
this avoids a war,

01:10:46.710 --> 01:10:48.470
this avoids
misunderstanding and so on.

01:10:48.470 --> 01:10:50.340
This is not true at all.

01:10:50.340 --> 01:10:53.580
We have the example, to
cite a very recent one,

01:10:53.580 --> 01:10:57.330
of former Yugoslavia in which
the Croats and the Serbs

01:10:57.330 --> 01:11:01.715
have fought violently
against each other

01:11:01.715 --> 01:11:03.840
although they speak more
or less the same language.

01:11:03.840 --> 01:11:06.780
Today I receive many
letters, threatening letters,

01:11:06.780 --> 01:11:09.540
from Zagreb, the
capital of Croatia,

01:11:09.540 --> 01:11:12.090
saying, please, Professor
Hagege, please all of you

01:11:12.090 --> 01:11:15.600
Western linguists, stops
speaking of Serbo-Croation.

01:11:15.600 --> 01:11:18.810
We do because Serbo-Croatian--
although for reasons

01:11:18.810 --> 01:11:21.540
of acceptable
nationalism-- the Croats

01:11:21.540 --> 01:11:24.480
want to individualize
themselves and to be considered

01:11:24.480 --> 01:11:26.110
a speaker of another language.

01:11:26.110 --> 01:11:31.530
It is true that Serbo-Croatian
is a language, a unified one,

01:11:31.530 --> 01:11:34.620
if we use a criterion
[INAUDIBLE] communication.

01:11:34.620 --> 01:11:37.110
Serbs can communicate
in Serb with Croats,

01:11:37.110 --> 01:11:38.370
communicating in Croat.

01:11:38.370 --> 01:11:41.040
It is, of course, one
and the same language

01:11:41.040 --> 01:11:45.340
which has regional variants
like any other human language.

01:11:45.340 --> 01:11:48.270
So they speak the same language.

01:11:48.270 --> 01:11:51.000
However, this does not
stop them from fighting

01:11:51.000 --> 01:11:56.730
and from separating
from each other

01:11:56.730 --> 01:11:58.600
in the beginning of the '90s.

01:11:58.600 --> 01:12:02.090
And this was a very fierce
war of which you are aware,

01:12:02.090 --> 01:12:02.800
all of you.

01:12:02.800 --> 01:12:05.910
So this is just one example
which, for lack of time,

01:12:05.910 --> 01:12:10.470
I give in order to demonstrate
that it is not true

01:12:10.470 --> 01:12:15.570
that one and the same
language is a promise of peace

01:12:15.570 --> 01:12:19.350
or this is an illusion and
a prejudice against which

01:12:19.350 --> 01:12:21.360
I must react.

01:12:21.360 --> 01:12:25.890
So in what case
could English become

01:12:25.890 --> 01:12:28.770
a real danger for languages?

01:12:28.770 --> 01:12:30.360
Just in one case.

01:12:30.360 --> 01:12:34.194
If English, not content
with being what it is now

01:12:34.194 --> 01:12:35.860
in many countries in
the world with what

01:12:35.860 --> 01:12:37.401
I said at the
beginning of this talk,

01:12:37.401 --> 01:12:40.900
I mean a second language,
an indigenized variety

01:12:40.900 --> 01:12:44.380
which could exist side by
side with the vernacular

01:12:44.380 --> 01:12:45.760
languages of the people.

01:12:45.760 --> 01:12:48.010
If English, not content
with being this,

01:12:48.010 --> 01:12:54.130
was substituted for the
other ones in private life,

01:12:54.130 --> 01:12:59.440
in the homes of every
family, if English became

01:12:59.440 --> 01:13:01.750
from the present state
of a second language

01:13:01.750 --> 01:13:04.730
in many countries,
which, as I said,

01:13:04.730 --> 01:13:07.210
reach much more English
speakers than there wants to,

01:13:07.210 --> 01:13:10.840
to whom it is native, if it
became the first language

01:13:10.840 --> 01:13:12.280
of all these countries.

01:13:12.280 --> 01:13:17.950
In other words, if English
knew the same future

01:13:17.950 --> 01:13:22.850
as Latin did when it
was substituted, when

01:13:22.850 --> 01:13:25.960
the languages of the
conquered Western Europe

01:13:25.960 --> 01:13:31.600
were substituted for by Latin,
Gaulish disappeared that way.

01:13:31.600 --> 01:13:33.160
Etruscan also.

01:13:33.160 --> 01:13:37.532
Dacian in present day
Romania, which was then Dacia,

01:13:37.532 --> 01:13:38.680
disappeared in that way.

01:13:38.680 --> 01:13:40.480
We do not know any
word of these languages

01:13:40.480 --> 01:13:43.870
because Latin replaced
them, and Latin

01:13:43.870 --> 01:13:46.660
was introduced not only
in public life, not

01:13:46.660 --> 01:13:49.640
in the streets, but
also in private life.

01:13:49.640 --> 01:13:53.170
So the problem-- this will
be my latest conclusion--

01:13:53.170 --> 01:13:59.710
is to what extent can English be
an example of the same process?

01:13:59.710 --> 01:14:02.560
I mean, is English, in
the present situation

01:14:02.560 --> 01:14:08.770
of the world, a language, which
can replace, by suppression,

01:14:08.770 --> 01:14:11.740
by being substituted
for all these languages,

01:14:11.740 --> 01:14:16.450
other languages which are
native to the people of most

01:14:16.450 --> 01:14:17.140
countries?

01:14:17.140 --> 01:14:19.840
I must say in this
case only would I

01:14:19.840 --> 01:14:22.930
accept to say that
English is a threat

01:14:22.930 --> 01:14:25.420
to the languages in question.

01:14:25.420 --> 01:14:27.400
I think that is not
the case, and I am not

01:14:27.400 --> 01:14:29.230
sure it will ever be the case.

01:14:29.230 --> 01:14:31.395
Thank you very much.

01:14:31.395 --> 01:14:37.830
[APPLAUSE]

01:14:37.830 --> 01:14:39.081
I'm sorry for taking too long.

01:14:39.081 --> 01:14:40.621
PRESENTER: Professor
Hagege, will you

01:14:40.621 --> 01:14:41.940
entertain a few questions?

01:14:41.940 --> 01:14:42.900
HAGEGE: Yes.

01:14:42.900 --> 01:14:46.350
AUDIENCE: I have an
observation and a question.

01:14:46.350 --> 01:14:51.720
The observation relates to
your discussion of Fishman

01:14:51.720 --> 01:15:01.800
and his list of adjectives to
describe French versus English.

01:15:01.800 --> 01:15:04.470
It occurs to me, as I was
listening to that list,

01:15:04.470 --> 01:15:09.210
that it corresponds very
closely to stereotypical notions

01:15:09.210 --> 01:15:11.520
of masculinity
versus femininity,

01:15:11.520 --> 01:15:19.590
with France, of course, being
the feminine marked object.

01:15:19.590 --> 01:15:23.210
In listening to that,
or thinking that,

01:15:23.210 --> 01:15:27.420
it reminded me of the
degree to which Germany,

01:15:27.420 --> 01:15:32.580
during the Nazi period,
did indeed conceive,

01:15:32.580 --> 01:15:38.730
in its political imaginary,
of France as a female vessel,

01:15:38.730 --> 01:15:42.410
in effect, or series
to its own vulcan,

01:15:42.410 --> 01:15:48.120
to use the ancient god imagery.

01:15:48.120 --> 01:15:51.550
And I find it somewhat
ironic that Fishman is a Jew,

01:15:51.550 --> 01:15:56.670
and that he's applying
these perhaps--

01:15:56.670 --> 01:16:00.000
or it seems with no
self-consciousness,

01:16:00.000 --> 01:16:03.480
which leads me to my question,
which is totally naive.

01:16:03.480 --> 01:16:07.949
In your discipline
of sociolinguistics--

01:16:07.949 --> 01:16:09.240
specifically sociolinguistics--

01:16:09.240 --> 01:16:10.410
HAGEGE: And also
linguistics proper.

01:16:10.410 --> 01:16:10.680
AUDIENCE: Yes.

01:16:10.680 --> 01:16:11.179
Yes.

01:16:11.179 --> 01:16:14.940
Well, then I said sub-discipline
of sociolinguistics.

01:16:14.940 --> 01:16:20.880
Is there a German branch, in
the 20th century, of scholarship

01:16:20.880 --> 01:16:26.040
that has contributed
to the sub-discipline?

01:16:26.040 --> 01:16:32.610
Does some of it date from before
the conclusion of World War II,

01:16:32.610 --> 01:16:36.660
and to what extent do
you as sociolinguists

01:16:36.660 --> 01:16:42.270
embrace, or have you rejected,
that sector of scholarship?

01:16:42.270 --> 01:16:44.620
HAGEGE: Well, it's
a good question.

01:16:44.620 --> 01:16:46.860
You said that the
adjectives which

01:16:46.860 --> 01:16:50.100
qualify French and English
in the text I mentioned

01:16:50.100 --> 01:16:54.930
from Joshua Fishman, represent
[INAUDIBLE] thinking, which,

01:16:54.930 --> 01:16:57.270
of course, is my opinion, too.

01:16:57.270 --> 01:16:59.050
I must say that if I
were a woman myself--

01:16:59.050 --> 01:17:01.160
which is not the
case, as you see--

01:17:01.160 --> 01:17:04.260
I would think that this is an
unacceptable, anti-feminist,

01:17:04.260 --> 01:17:06.150
and [INAUDIBLE]
view because it's

01:17:06.150 --> 01:17:09.180
quite ridiculous to associate
certain things with women

01:17:09.180 --> 01:17:10.680
and other ones with men.

01:17:10.680 --> 01:17:13.800
Women are not necessarily more
tender or more and so on--more

01:17:13.800 --> 01:17:16.052
feminine in the masculine
sense of the word.

01:17:16.052 --> 01:17:17.260
And men are not the contrary.

01:17:17.260 --> 01:17:20.790
This is quite an inspiration,
which is quite anti-feminist,

01:17:20.790 --> 01:17:23.670
which I can't accept.

01:17:23.670 --> 01:17:29.760
Now what is behind this
kind of inspiration?

01:17:29.760 --> 01:17:33.960
You ask whether I know of
German researchers, scholars

01:17:33.960 --> 01:17:36.014
who purported such a view.

01:17:36.014 --> 01:17:36.930
Is that your question?

01:17:36.930 --> 01:17:38.470
AUDIENCE: Well, I'm just
wondering what the state--

01:17:38.470 --> 01:17:39.210
I have no idea.

01:17:39.210 --> 01:17:41.190
I'm not familiar with
the discipline at all.

01:17:41.190 --> 01:17:45.720
So I'm just wondering, from
a historic point of view,

01:17:45.720 --> 01:17:51.120
whether the contributions
of German scholarship

01:17:51.120 --> 01:17:54.021
pre-World War II to your field.

01:17:54.021 --> 01:17:56.520
HAGEGE: As soon as the end of
the 18th century and beginning

01:17:56.520 --> 01:18:00.930
of the Romantic period,
nouns such as [INAUDIBLE],,

01:18:00.930 --> 01:18:03.060
the Grimm Brothers,
the Schlegels,

01:18:03.060 --> 01:18:06.900
and many of the well-known
German scholars-- who,

01:18:06.900 --> 01:18:09.960
by the way, were the founders
of linguistics and the ones who

01:18:09.960 --> 01:18:13.140
used the word for the
first time in history--

01:18:13.140 --> 01:18:15.180
had a certain
tendency, that's true,

01:18:15.180 --> 01:18:21.180
to associate language with
certain psychological, cultural

01:18:21.180 --> 01:18:22.020
settings.

01:18:22.020 --> 01:18:24.150
And this is a thinking
which, of course,

01:18:24.150 --> 01:18:27.870
today appears as completely
obsolete and reactionary.

01:18:27.870 --> 01:18:30.600
But it dominated German
thinking during a long time.

01:18:30.600 --> 01:18:32.190
This is true.

01:18:32.190 --> 01:18:34.480
AUDIENCE: It connects
with a certain metaphorics

01:18:34.480 --> 01:18:35.571
that one uses.

01:18:35.571 --> 01:18:37.820
HAGEGE: Its formation was
[INAUDIBLE] metaphoric, yes.

01:18:37.820 --> 01:18:39.945
AUDIENCE: In light of your
presentation last night,

01:18:39.945 --> 01:18:42.930
in which you yourself
adopted a 19th century--

01:18:42.930 --> 01:18:44.160
HAGEGE: In another context.

01:18:44.160 --> 01:18:45.660
AUDIENCE: Right,
in another context,

01:18:45.660 --> 01:18:49.890
a 19th century
metaphor of vitalism,

01:18:49.890 --> 01:18:52.350
fully acknowledging
that it didn't fit.

01:18:52.350 --> 01:18:56.580
I'm wondering if your
sub-discipline inevitably

01:18:56.580 --> 01:19:01.710
has to be drawn to metaphors
which are ideologically

01:19:01.710 --> 01:19:03.250
and politically freighted.

01:19:05.890 --> 01:19:09.730
HAGEGE: I can mention a fact,
which probably you know,

01:19:09.730 --> 01:19:13.180
as a testimony of the
importance of the way

01:19:13.180 --> 01:19:16.360
a political power
views language.

01:19:16.360 --> 01:19:20.290
I have documents, which
I found some years ago,

01:19:20.290 --> 01:19:23.870
on Hitler's
attitudes to Germany.

01:19:23.870 --> 01:19:27.280
He was, as you know, Austrian,
and he had a very strong

01:19:27.280 --> 01:19:29.800
Austrian accent.

01:19:29.800 --> 01:19:31.150
He has a very accurate voice.

01:19:31.150 --> 01:19:35.680
I have heard his
voice on records.

01:19:35.680 --> 01:19:41.380
And his Austrian accent was
jeered at the beginning,

01:19:41.380 --> 01:19:47.710
jeered at, mocked, laughed
at, and ridiculed--

01:19:47.710 --> 01:19:52.660
at the beginning when he did not
have power until quite later.

01:19:52.660 --> 01:19:55.810
And as soon as he became
more and more powerful,

01:19:55.810 --> 01:19:58.600
all the Germans who did not
pronounce German as he did

01:19:58.600 --> 01:19:59.950
imitate his pronunciation.

01:19:59.950 --> 01:20:04.780
And in articles I have, we see
that not only in phonetics,

01:20:04.780 --> 01:20:07.450
but also in morphology
and in the lexicon,

01:20:07.450 --> 01:20:11.050
many words which
were Hitler words

01:20:11.050 --> 01:20:14.240
were widespread in Germany
during the whole Nazi period.

01:20:14.240 --> 01:20:16.330
So I just mention this fact.

01:20:16.330 --> 01:20:20.120
I don't to what extent it
responds to your query.

01:20:20.120 --> 01:20:23.290
But I just mean by that
it is quite possible

01:20:23.290 --> 01:20:26.770
to politicize a form,
a norm of a language,

01:20:26.770 --> 01:20:29.980
through a certain
regime, and to associate

01:20:29.980 --> 01:20:33.520
a certain conception of the
language to a certain regime.

01:20:33.520 --> 01:20:37.480
This is which it is little
known, but assessed,

01:20:37.480 --> 01:20:38.390
and it existed.

01:20:38.390 --> 01:20:43.450
This is what took place
during the Second World War.

01:20:43.450 --> 01:20:46.110
To what extent does that
correspond to your--

01:20:46.110 --> 01:20:49.400
AUDIENCE: Well, it just adds
a level of interest, I think.

01:20:53.150 --> 01:20:53.650
HAGEGE: Yes.

01:20:57.055 --> 01:20:59.680
AUDIENCE: Actually, I wanted to
ask you about a couple of words

01:20:59.680 --> 01:21:01.820
that you ed yourself.

01:21:01.820 --> 01:21:07.030
And I recognize one of
them, maybe someone--

01:21:07.030 --> 01:21:11.860
did you write the translation
that you were reading of--

01:21:11.860 --> 01:21:16.320
anyway, one word
is passive, which

01:21:16.320 --> 01:21:19.530
relates to what Edward
was talking about.

01:21:19.530 --> 01:21:24.220
That is, when you use the
word passive, which is really

01:21:24.220 --> 01:21:28.710
a sexualized metaphor to
refer to the condition

01:21:28.710 --> 01:21:30.990
of a language getting
a lot of loan words

01:21:30.990 --> 01:21:32.720
from another language.

01:21:32.720 --> 01:21:34.470
HAGEGE: Did I use the
word passive myself?

01:21:34.470 --> 01:21:36.610
AUDIENCE: Yes, you did.

01:21:36.610 --> 01:21:38.820
And in particular,
for example, that's

01:21:38.820 --> 01:21:42.420
not a metaphor that
normally is used

01:21:42.420 --> 01:21:46.540
to describe the post-1066
period in English,

01:21:46.540 --> 01:21:49.680
when I think many more
words came in from French

01:21:49.680 --> 01:21:53.760
than ever have come into
French from English.

01:21:53.760 --> 01:21:58.200
And the other word I wondered
if you would talk about

01:21:58.200 --> 01:22:01.470
was one you used earlier
in your talk, which

01:22:01.470 --> 01:22:04.750
is vulgar, where you--

01:22:04.750 --> 01:22:06.450
HAGEGE: Quite subjective.

01:22:06.450 --> 01:22:09.330
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] where
you characterize the sound

01:22:09.330 --> 01:22:12.090
of American English as vulgar.

01:22:12.090 --> 01:22:16.320
And I wondered how that matched
up with your characterization

01:22:16.320 --> 01:22:20.080
of yourself as on the left.

01:22:20.080 --> 01:22:21.670
HAGEGE: No, the
reason is very simple.

01:22:21.670 --> 01:22:22.870
Not far to seek.

01:22:22.870 --> 01:22:25.280
I learned English from
British professors.

01:22:25.280 --> 01:22:29.230
So the way they pronounced was
the one I tried to imitate.

01:22:29.230 --> 01:22:31.270
And when I went for the
first time in America,

01:22:31.270 --> 01:22:33.519
I was startled by the way
Americans pronounced English

01:22:33.519 --> 01:22:34.750
because it was not mine.

01:22:34.750 --> 01:22:37.370
So my reaction-- vulgar,
for which I apologize,

01:22:37.370 --> 01:22:38.800
was its integration.

01:22:38.800 --> 01:22:41.350
And I don't want
to be aggressive.

01:22:41.350 --> 01:22:43.052
It was the way I
reacted to this kind--

01:22:43.052 --> 01:22:45.510
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] that as
a register for characterizing

01:22:45.510 --> 01:22:47.650
the way the
differences in speech

01:22:47.650 --> 01:22:51.920
strikes me as something that
is a problem if you think

01:22:51.920 --> 01:22:53.460
of yourself as [INAUDIBLE].

01:22:53.460 --> 01:22:55.360
HAGEGE: No, vulgar is a word--

01:22:55.360 --> 01:22:57.180
I am quite ready
to recognize-- it

01:22:57.180 --> 01:22:59.880
is a word which is completely
subjective as I use it,

01:22:59.880 --> 01:23:02.030
as I said, to refer it
to a part of my biography

01:23:02.030 --> 01:23:04.740
in which I discovered the
way Americans pronounce

01:23:04.740 --> 01:23:08.460
English, which was not the one
I have been taught that well,

01:23:08.460 --> 01:23:10.020
is the most widespread today.

01:23:10.020 --> 01:23:13.830
But I grew up in
another kind of English,

01:23:13.830 --> 01:23:16.500
although, as you
may observe, many

01:23:16.500 --> 01:23:21.080
of my own way of
pronouncing are more or less

01:23:21.080 --> 01:23:23.370
inspired by the American
way of pronouncing.

01:23:23.370 --> 01:23:25.210
Well, the word vulgar
is not a good word.

01:23:25.210 --> 01:23:30.270
And I do not use it
as a scientific word.

01:23:30.270 --> 01:23:31.770
It's quite a subjective one.

01:23:31.770 --> 01:23:33.886
As far as passive
is concerned, I

01:23:33.886 --> 01:23:35.760
would probably be more
reluctant, more loathe

01:23:35.760 --> 01:23:39.940
to reject it for a
very simple reason.

01:23:39.940 --> 01:23:42.180
It's just a matter
of terminology.

01:23:42.180 --> 01:23:45.270
If by passive we mean a language
which accepts or receives

01:23:45.270 --> 01:23:48.000
a great, huge amount
of borrowed words,

01:23:48.000 --> 01:23:52.660
then the word is not marked more
than just referring to that.

01:23:52.660 --> 01:23:56.850
And we have many languages
which, in this respect,

01:23:56.850 --> 01:23:57.360
passive--

01:23:57.360 --> 01:23:59.970
and if you want to avoid
the implications of the word

01:23:59.970 --> 01:24:04.550
passive, we will just say
open to borrowing words, which

01:24:04.550 --> 01:24:05.800
is the case of many languages.

01:24:05.800 --> 01:24:06.841
Other ones, as you know--

01:24:06.841 --> 01:24:08.100
Icelandic, for example.

01:24:08.100 --> 01:24:09.450
Fijian.

01:24:09.450 --> 01:24:13.020
By the way, both of them
are language of islanders--

01:24:13.020 --> 01:24:16.170
are not passive in this respect
because they do not borrow,

01:24:16.170 --> 01:24:19.380
and they are also
watched by purists

01:24:19.380 --> 01:24:24.780
who want to tap the original
fund in order for them

01:24:24.780 --> 01:24:25.980
to find neologisms.

01:24:25.980 --> 01:24:28.470
They do not want to borrow.

01:24:28.470 --> 01:24:30.210
So there is a choice.

01:24:30.210 --> 01:24:31.890
There are two adages in neology.

01:24:31.890 --> 01:24:35.220
Either we borrow great
matches of words to translate

01:24:35.220 --> 01:24:37.680
[INAUDIBLE] which correspond
to the modern technical word,

01:24:37.680 --> 01:24:45.120
or we exclusively resort to
words from the original funds.

01:24:45.120 --> 01:24:48.447
In the case of
Icelandic, Old Norse,

01:24:48.447 --> 01:24:50.530
which is the origin of all
Scandinavian languages.

01:24:50.530 --> 01:24:53.640
[INAUDIBLE],, as the case of
Arabic. it is the same thing.

01:24:53.640 --> 01:24:59.130
And I sometimes cite
Israeli Hebrew and Arabic,

01:24:59.130 --> 01:25:01.410
in this respect, are
very characteristic.

01:25:01.410 --> 01:25:06.810
When it came to
designating atomic bomb,

01:25:06.810 --> 01:25:11.290
the Arabian scholars who
were asked by the governments

01:25:11.290 --> 01:25:14.301
to look for a word-- because the
Koran does not speak of atomic

01:25:14.301 --> 01:25:14.800
bomb--

01:25:17.320 --> 01:25:19.660
does someone speak Arabic here?

01:25:19.660 --> 01:25:24.040
They took a root from the Koran,
they took [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH],,

01:25:24.040 --> 01:25:27.180
which means a parcel,
because the prophet says

01:25:27.180 --> 01:25:28.620
[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH].

01:25:37.330 --> 01:25:39.670
In this context,
the prophet said,

01:25:39.670 --> 01:25:44.860
you will not bring any parcel
of good to the paradise

01:25:44.860 --> 01:25:49.510
if you don't follow my religion.

01:25:49.510 --> 01:25:52.150
And the word parcel,
which is a Quranic word,

01:25:52.150 --> 01:25:54.370
and to that extent
very prestigious,

01:25:54.370 --> 01:25:59.950
was chosen by the Arabic
scholars to express atomic.

01:25:59.950 --> 01:26:02.710
Of course, it was not a good
word because atomic is a Greek

01:26:02.710 --> 01:26:05.915
word which means which
cannot be cut more.

01:26:05.915 --> 01:26:08.830
[INAUDIBLE] is privative, and
[INAUDIBLE] is [INAUDIBLE],,

01:26:08.830 --> 01:26:11.700
the Greek [INAUDIBLE] means
a thing which you cannot cut

01:26:11.700 --> 01:26:12.430
further.

01:26:12.430 --> 01:26:16.000
It is the last result
of a cutting operation.

01:26:16.000 --> 01:26:19.060
But the Arabians thought
that they had to,

01:26:19.060 --> 01:26:22.570
instead of borrowing
the word atomic bomb,

01:26:22.570 --> 01:26:26.080
to take a word from the Koran
because it is respectable,

01:26:26.080 --> 01:26:28.840
whereas the Israelis adopted--

01:26:28.840 --> 01:26:33.340
was just a kind of phonetic
transformation-- bomba atomica

01:26:33.340 --> 01:26:35.600
without any further ado.

01:26:35.600 --> 01:26:39.940
This means that there is a
kind of political attitude

01:26:39.940 --> 01:26:41.980
behind the choice of neologisms.

01:26:41.980 --> 01:26:45.640
Either we are passive in the
sense which I [INAUDIBLE]

01:26:45.640 --> 01:26:48.130
myself to use, and
means open to borrowing

01:26:48.130 --> 01:26:51.340
without much hesitation,
not reluctant,

01:26:51.340 --> 01:26:56.630
or we consider that neology
is, to a large extent,

01:26:56.630 --> 01:26:58.540
a political activity,
and there is

01:26:58.540 --> 01:27:01.630
a national or nationalist
implication behind.

01:27:01.630 --> 01:27:04.290
And this is what many
languages in the modern world

01:27:04.290 --> 01:27:07.737
give us examples
of, I would say.

01:27:07.737 --> 01:27:08.320
AUDIENCE: Yes.

01:27:08.320 --> 01:27:08.820
Hi.

01:27:08.820 --> 01:27:11.140
I guess I have one
comment and two questions.

01:27:11.140 --> 01:27:17.150
The comment that English
is not a threat insofar

01:27:17.150 --> 01:27:19.580
as people are not giving
up local languages

01:27:19.580 --> 01:27:21.790
and taking on
English is certainly

01:27:21.790 --> 01:27:23.740
false in the case of
the United States,

01:27:23.740 --> 01:27:28.630
where so many Native American
languages are disappearing.

01:27:28.630 --> 01:27:30.700
The late Ken Hale, who
was a linguist here,

01:27:30.700 --> 01:27:33.800
spent his life trying to
rescue many of these languages,

01:27:33.800 --> 01:27:35.099
a difficult struggle.

01:27:35.099 --> 01:27:36.890
It is a threat, certainly,
in this country.

01:27:36.890 --> 01:27:38.390
HAGEGE: I did not refer to
that because it was the subject

01:27:38.390 --> 01:27:39.780
on my talk of yesterday.

01:27:39.780 --> 01:27:41.170
AUDIENCE: I wasn't at the talk.

01:27:41.170 --> 01:27:43.130
HAGEGE: I devoted it exclusively
to the subject about treating.

01:27:43.130 --> 01:27:45.740
So I spoke about American
Indian languages disappearing.

01:27:45.740 --> 01:27:46.698
AUDIENCE: Oh, I'm glad.

01:27:46.698 --> 01:27:47.500
Thank you.

01:27:47.500 --> 01:27:51.820
The question is you described
those words in Japanese

01:27:51.820 --> 01:27:53.680
as Japanized English.

01:27:53.680 --> 01:27:56.170
But not really
Japanized English.

01:27:56.170 --> 01:27:59.890
This is English words being
adapted to the Japanese system,

01:27:59.890 --> 01:28:02.920
and thoroughly adapted
to it, to such an extent

01:28:02.920 --> 01:28:06.280
that many Japanese don't even
know that these terms came

01:28:06.280 --> 01:28:07.990
from English in the end.

01:28:07.990 --> 01:28:11.020
The question to me is that the
Japanese are not particularly

01:28:11.020 --> 01:28:12.020
concerned about this.

01:28:12.020 --> 01:28:14.510
There's not much
opposition to this.

01:28:14.510 --> 01:28:17.270
There was in the 1930s
in the military regime

01:28:17.270 --> 01:28:19.960
where they did make
this effort to create

01:28:19.960 --> 01:28:23.670
scientific, technological
terms from old Chinese roots.

01:28:23.670 --> 01:28:26.980
But unlike the French, the
Japanese are not concerned.

01:28:26.980 --> 01:28:27.847
HAGEGE: They're not?

01:28:27.847 --> 01:28:29.930
AUDIENCE: They are not
concerned about this influx

01:28:29.930 --> 01:28:32.470
of what we think of as English
words into the vocabulary.

01:28:32.470 --> 01:28:33.220
HAGEGE: It's true.

01:28:33.220 --> 01:28:33.719
It's true.

01:28:33.719 --> 01:28:37.280
AUDIENCE: And I wonder if
you have a comment on that.

01:28:37.280 --> 01:28:40.280
The other question is the
change of syntax in Chinese,

01:28:40.280 --> 01:28:44.050
which is quite interesting
because, again, it complicates

01:28:44.050 --> 01:28:46.390
the political question.

01:28:46.390 --> 01:28:49.190
Chinese in general does not
try to sound out English words.

01:28:49.190 --> 01:28:51.350
They look for Chinese
roots for new terms.

01:28:51.350 --> 01:28:55.210
And yet, as you know, some of
Chinese syntax and word order

01:28:55.210 --> 01:28:58.160
is now being affected
by English influences.

01:28:58.160 --> 01:28:59.980
This is a subtler difference.

01:28:59.980 --> 01:29:02.440
I haven't seen Chinese
object to this,

01:29:02.440 --> 01:29:04.060
but they do know it's happening.

01:29:04.060 --> 01:29:06.662
Are there other examples of
this kind of syntactic change

01:29:06.662 --> 01:29:07.720
in other places?

01:29:07.720 --> 01:29:08.560
HAGEGE: Yes.

01:29:08.560 --> 01:29:13.551
To begin with the end, and
concerning the Chinese syntax,

01:29:13.551 --> 01:29:15.300
you are a sinologist
yourself, aren't you?

01:29:15.300 --> 01:29:16.040
AUDIENCE: Yes.

01:29:16.040 --> 01:29:17.873
HAGEGE: You know that
[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH],,

01:29:17.873 --> 01:29:23.370
meaning and, is not normally
used in traditional Chinese

01:29:23.370 --> 01:29:29.740
every time we conjunct things,
and is not as often used

01:29:29.740 --> 01:29:32.740
as the and and the A
of English and French.

01:29:32.740 --> 01:29:37.810
And now we have texts which are
full of [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH],,

01:29:37.810 --> 01:29:40.750
whereas the [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]
of genuine Chinese is

01:29:40.750 --> 01:29:42.460
a preposition meaning with.

01:29:42.460 --> 01:29:49.709
And [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]
agreeing with him.

01:29:49.709 --> 01:29:51.750
And this [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH],,
which is Chinese,

01:29:51.750 --> 01:29:55.430
yields today a conjunction of
coordination, which did exist,

01:29:55.430 --> 01:29:58.330
in fact, in Chinese, but
on a much lesser extent,

01:29:58.330 --> 01:30:01.335
and which is pervading--
invading the Chinese syntax

01:30:01.335 --> 01:30:01.960
and morphology.

01:30:01.960 --> 01:30:03.668
This is an example
among many other ones.

01:30:03.668 --> 01:30:05.850
Another one,
[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH] which are

01:30:05.850 --> 01:30:10.740
the determiner in
the noun group.

01:30:10.740 --> 01:30:12.730
I say [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH].

01:30:16.430 --> 01:30:22.570
The book, I read
the book I wrote.

01:30:22.570 --> 01:30:26.510
In Chinese, when we have
between the subject [INAUDIBLE],,

01:30:26.510 --> 01:30:29.450
or no subject, which is
not it is often left away,

01:30:29.450 --> 01:30:31.974
and the [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH],,
a very long stretch of words,

01:30:31.974 --> 01:30:34.140
the Chinese avoid having
this [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH],,

01:30:34.140 --> 01:30:38.480
and they permute and
choose another structure.

01:30:38.480 --> 01:30:42.130
But Chinese, which is
[INAUDIBLE] on English,

01:30:42.130 --> 01:30:45.146
has very, very long
relative prepositions with

01:30:45.146 --> 01:30:47.020
[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH],,
which are not Chinese.

01:30:47.020 --> 01:30:48.730
And I have many
examples of that,

01:30:48.730 --> 01:30:51.070
and I was referring
to that exactly.

01:30:51.070 --> 01:30:53.687
Now as far as the first part
of your question is concerned,

01:30:53.687 --> 01:30:54.520
you are quite right.

01:30:54.520 --> 01:30:58.480
And I would accept that I
exaggerated, to some extent,

01:30:58.480 --> 01:31:02.950
and should not speak so much
of Americanized Japanese,

01:31:02.950 --> 01:31:08.980
Japanese as a very large
amount of borrowings.

01:31:08.980 --> 01:31:11.320
I will say I am
not a specialist.

01:31:11.320 --> 01:31:12.610
I speak Japanese fluently,.

01:31:12.610 --> 01:31:16.750
But what I see when I give talks
in Japanese in Japan when I am

01:31:16.750 --> 01:31:19.960
invited-- when I speak
with the people later--

01:31:19.960 --> 01:31:22.480
what I see is that
I would strongly

01:31:22.480 --> 01:31:26.080
tend to think that there is a
continuity in Japanese history,

01:31:26.080 --> 01:31:29.530
just the same way as in the
sixth century, this country

01:31:29.530 --> 01:31:31.660
of Samurais, which
was illiterate,

01:31:31.660 --> 01:31:34.350
adopted Chinese characters,
just in the same way

01:31:34.350 --> 01:31:37.480
as it adopted an enormous
amount of Chinese

01:31:37.480 --> 01:31:39.900
by syllable which it
pronounced the Japanese way,

01:31:39.900 --> 01:31:42.490
but which way what we
call Sino-Japanese.

01:31:42.490 --> 01:31:46.090
In the same way, recently,
it has adopted a great,

01:31:46.090 --> 01:31:51.670
great many English borrowed
words, which lead me to think--

01:31:51.670 --> 01:31:55.360
which is a hypothesis which
has a cultural or sociocultural

01:31:55.360 --> 01:31:56.790
and philosophical implication--

01:31:56.790 --> 01:31:58.630
I am not a philosopher
and don't want

01:31:58.630 --> 01:32:03.490
to indulge in a
facilities or things which

01:32:03.490 --> 01:32:04.600
could be contradicted.

01:32:04.600 --> 01:32:07.420
But it leads me to think
that, as opposed to French,

01:32:07.420 --> 01:32:08.990
as you said yourself,
and you say,

01:32:08.990 --> 01:32:13.150
unlike French, in which language
is a very politicized and very

01:32:13.150 --> 01:32:16.870
nationalistic thing
of which French

01:32:16.870 --> 01:32:20.710
have a very high conscience,
and which has always

01:32:20.710 --> 01:32:24.580
been associated with power
under the monarchy just the same

01:32:24.580 --> 01:32:25.840
as any other republic.

01:32:25.840 --> 01:32:28.300
On the contrary-- contrary
to that, in Japan--

01:32:28.300 --> 01:32:31.630
it seems to me that language
is not a political matter.

01:32:31.630 --> 01:32:34.225
In France it is a very
strongly political matter,

01:32:34.225 --> 01:32:36.100
and the reaction of many
French intellectuals

01:32:36.100 --> 01:32:38.860
against Americanization
is directly

01:32:38.860 --> 01:32:42.370
drawn from this psychology,
which in the case of Japan,

01:32:42.370 --> 01:32:43.580
is almost non-existent.

01:32:43.580 --> 01:32:45.738
This is what I would propose.

01:32:45.738 --> 01:32:47.196
PRESENTER: Maybe
one more question?

01:32:47.196 --> 01:32:49.670
Yes.

01:32:49.670 --> 01:32:54.470
AUDIENCE: I'm going to make
an observation, a question

01:32:54.470 --> 01:32:56.450
from a different
perspective, which

01:32:56.450 --> 01:32:59.750
is I think connected to
us, our being at MIT,

01:32:59.750 --> 01:33:05.360
which is you were talking about
the differences in English

01:33:05.360 --> 01:33:08.710
largely in terms of
different countries

01:33:08.710 --> 01:33:11.190
in terms of real space.

01:33:11.190 --> 01:33:11.910
HAGEGE: Of what?

01:33:11.910 --> 01:33:16.320
AUDIENCE: Of real space,
real geographic space.

01:33:16.320 --> 01:33:18.310
You mentioned a
little the internet.

01:33:18.310 --> 01:33:23.940
But think it's by no coincidence
that the worldwide web was

01:33:23.940 --> 01:33:25.950
invented by an
Englishman working

01:33:25.950 --> 01:33:29.445
in a Francophone city for
an international physics

01:33:29.445 --> 01:33:34.980
committee, and that one of
the ways that English really

01:33:34.980 --> 01:33:40.850
is becoming a world language is
within science and technology.

01:33:40.850 --> 01:33:46.640
And in certain fields, such
as physics, biology, computer

01:33:46.640 --> 01:33:53.150
science, the discourse is
really monolingual in English.

01:33:53.150 --> 01:33:57.620
And I was just wondering
what's your take on--

01:33:57.620 --> 01:34:00.260
and there's good reasons
for that in the sense

01:34:00.260 --> 01:34:02.990
that it has to be one
language because there

01:34:02.990 --> 01:34:08.300
is a premium placed on precision
and the ability to share data

01:34:08.300 --> 01:34:12.100
and all those kinds of things.

01:34:12.100 --> 01:34:16.690
But again, this is never
going to be a first language

01:34:16.690 --> 01:34:17.650
for these people.

01:34:17.650 --> 01:34:20.150
It's created a phenomenon
that we already

01:34:20.150 --> 01:34:24.830
have where the vast majority
of scientific and technical

01:34:24.830 --> 01:34:28.460
communication in the
world is in English.

01:34:28.460 --> 01:34:33.600
But the majority of people
who are reading and writing

01:34:33.600 --> 01:34:38.420
or receiving and
producing that language

01:34:38.420 --> 01:34:40.900
are not native speakers.

01:34:40.900 --> 01:34:43.050
And do you see that as a threat?

01:34:43.050 --> 01:34:43.650
HAGEGE: Oh.

01:34:43.650 --> 01:34:46.230
I had a question.

01:34:46.230 --> 01:34:49.950
Are you able to appreciate the
quality and the correctness

01:34:49.950 --> 01:34:53.860
of their English, these scholars
who are not native speakers?

01:34:53.860 --> 01:34:57.690
And do you not think that,
as the text I read strongly

01:34:57.690 --> 01:35:01.680
suggests, there is an unequal
situation because many of these

01:35:01.680 --> 01:35:05.310
non-native speakers of English
who, in scientific congress,

01:35:05.310 --> 01:35:09.000
resort to it, cannot express
their thought as fully as they

01:35:09.000 --> 01:35:11.330
would if they used
their native tongues?

01:35:11.330 --> 01:35:14.110
AUDIENCE: But they couldn't
communicate to each other.

01:35:14.110 --> 01:35:15.210
HAGEGE: I'm not sure.

01:35:15.210 --> 01:35:17.930
AUDIENCE: Well, actually,
I've talked to physicists,

01:35:17.930 --> 01:35:20.696
and they would say that they
can get much more work done

01:35:20.696 --> 01:35:23.190
[INAUDIBLE].

01:35:23.190 --> 01:35:26.760
HAGEGE: What gives you a good
argument-- which of course, I

01:35:26.760 --> 01:35:30.720
don't criticize-- is
that scientific scholars

01:35:30.720 --> 01:35:34.100
and scientists have always
had an international language.

01:35:34.100 --> 01:35:35.640
It was Latin.

01:35:35.640 --> 01:35:38.190
For some time it was German,
yes, and today it's English.

01:35:38.190 --> 01:35:39.330
It's true.

01:35:39.330 --> 01:35:43.710
But Latin was a dead
language already.

01:35:43.710 --> 01:35:47.250
I mean, it did not exist anymore
in everyday conversation.

01:35:47.250 --> 01:35:50.400
So it was unmarked in the
linguistic sense of unmarked.

01:35:50.400 --> 01:35:54.390
German reigned during a
very short period of time.

01:35:54.390 --> 01:35:57.690
And today the reign
of English is not

01:35:57.690 --> 01:36:00.540
without causing some
political problems.

01:36:00.540 --> 01:36:03.750
This is the reason
why I am not quite--

01:36:03.750 --> 01:36:06.810
although I'm not
reluctant, not loathe to--

01:36:06.810 --> 01:36:10.630
I'm not quite ready to adopt
it without some hesitation.

01:36:10.630 --> 01:36:12.978
There is something implied
behind that which is--

01:36:12.978 --> 01:36:14.728
AUDIENCE: Whatever
political [INAUDIBLE],,

01:36:14.728 --> 01:36:17.912
there's a huge critical
mass of the fact

01:36:17.912 --> 01:36:21.450
that the literature
in these fields

01:36:21.450 --> 01:36:24.890
is now being
produced in English.

01:36:24.890 --> 01:36:27.830
And that makes it,
in some ways, there

01:36:27.830 --> 01:36:34.490
is a huge amount of momentum
to keep it in English.

01:36:34.490 --> 01:36:35.400
HAGEGE: No.

01:36:35.400 --> 01:36:37.010
I would have a question.

01:36:37.010 --> 01:36:39.155
I think-- you're
not a journalist.

01:36:39.155 --> 01:36:40.340
AUDIENCE: No.

01:36:40.340 --> 01:36:42.380
HAGEGE: You are scholar.

01:36:42.380 --> 01:36:47.420
So I tell you why I opened
the question whether you might

01:36:47.420 --> 01:36:49.200
or might not be a journalist.

01:36:49.200 --> 01:36:52.590
I think this does
not apply to you.

01:36:52.590 --> 01:36:57.410
But in general, that there is
a kind of terrorist attitude

01:36:57.410 --> 01:36:59.330
when repeating,
repeating, repeating

01:36:59.330 --> 01:37:01.610
a thing which has
not been demonstrated

01:37:01.610 --> 01:37:02.660
by many observations.

01:37:02.660 --> 01:37:08.120
I mean, many people say what you
have just said, to what extent

01:37:08.120 --> 01:37:09.170
is it true?

01:37:09.170 --> 01:37:12.920
The ones who say what you
say, have these people

01:37:12.920 --> 01:37:15.470
a testimony of
all the congresses

01:37:15.470 --> 01:37:17.870
in a given subject
in which they have

01:37:17.870 --> 01:37:19.640
been able to
observe that English

01:37:19.640 --> 01:37:22.370
was the only used language?

01:37:22.370 --> 01:37:27.500
And to what extent is it not
a journalist, to some extent,

01:37:27.500 --> 01:37:30.500
terrorist attitude, the fact
of repeating, repeating,

01:37:30.500 --> 01:37:32.310
repeating the thesis,
the situation,

01:37:32.310 --> 01:37:34.820
that you cannot avoid it
and that you have to submit

01:37:34.820 --> 01:37:36.000
yourself to it?

01:37:36.000 --> 01:37:37.747
To this I react.

01:37:37.747 --> 01:37:39.830
AUDIENCE: Well, I can give
you an actual concrete.

01:37:39.830 --> 01:37:44.730
In physics, the language
of publication is LaTeX.

01:37:44.730 --> 01:37:45.980
HAGEGE: Is--?

01:37:45.980 --> 01:37:52.854
AUDIENCE: It's a typesetting,
word processing language.

01:37:52.854 --> 01:37:56.890
And all physicists
use LaTeX because it

01:37:56.890 --> 01:38:01.170
allows the use of the writing
equations [INAUDIBLE]..

01:38:01.170 --> 01:38:05.040
LaTeX is English-based.

01:38:05.040 --> 01:38:08.400
But basically, to publish in
physics-- and this is true--

01:38:08.400 --> 01:38:11.870
one has to-- especially
since the fall of the Soviet

01:38:11.870 --> 01:38:15.470
Union, when the only
other real alternative

01:38:15.470 --> 01:38:19.400
language for
physics was Russian.

01:38:19.400 --> 01:38:23.420
Since that point, just because
of the fact that, in essence,

01:38:23.420 --> 01:38:26.658
the only publication tools
in English-based publication,

01:38:26.658 --> 01:38:29.520
that it really is true.

01:38:29.520 --> 01:38:32.160
HAGEGE: Well, if it
is true, as you say,

01:38:32.160 --> 01:38:35.820
then don't you think that the
ones to whom English is not

01:38:35.820 --> 01:38:38.670
native, and who may
be great scholars,

01:38:38.670 --> 01:38:42.750
are in a situation of inequality
because they cannot express

01:38:42.750 --> 01:38:46.860
their thoughtfully, even though
you may purport that physics is

01:38:46.860 --> 01:38:50.880
not a literary matter, and that
you have just to expose what

01:38:50.880 --> 01:38:54.790
you have experience about, and
to speak in terms of numbers

01:38:54.790 --> 01:38:55.400
and so on.

01:38:55.400 --> 01:38:58.830
There is also a certain
discourse in physic.

01:38:58.830 --> 01:39:01.890
And this discourse
requires that you have

01:39:01.890 --> 01:39:03.420
a minimum mastering of English.

01:39:03.420 --> 01:39:06.490
And if you don't, this is an
unequal situation for scholars

01:39:06.490 --> 01:39:08.970
who are not English speakers.

01:39:08.970 --> 01:39:11.118
AUDIENCE: Can I ask
you one question?

01:39:11.118 --> 01:39:13.284
It's a question, also, to
follow up on your question

01:39:13.284 --> 01:39:13.910
that you asked.

01:39:13.910 --> 01:39:15.170
HAGEGE: This is an
important question.

01:39:15.170 --> 01:39:16.280
Thank you very much for--

01:39:16.280 --> 01:39:17.905
AUDIENCE: I'm curious,
as you yourself,

01:39:17.905 --> 01:39:19.710
you keep saying
that you cannot--

01:39:19.710 --> 01:39:20.953
if you express yourself
in another language,

01:39:20.953 --> 01:39:23.286
you cannot express yourself
fully, communicate as fully.

01:39:23.286 --> 01:39:29.100
Do you feel that way yourself,
that when you speak to us,

01:39:29.100 --> 01:39:32.480
do you feel that you're
not communicating as fully?

01:39:32.480 --> 01:39:33.772
HAGEGE: I'm not a good example.

01:39:33.772 --> 01:39:34.271
HAGEGE: OK.

01:39:34.271 --> 01:39:34.950
All right.

01:39:34.950 --> 01:39:37.280
So you would take a [INAUDIBLE].

01:39:37.280 --> 01:39:38.140
HAGEGE: I thinK--

01:39:38.140 --> 01:39:42.230
I am a-- if I can
use this neologism--

01:39:42.230 --> 01:39:45.095
a hyper verbalized man.

01:39:45.095 --> 01:39:46.970
And to the extent that
I am hyper verbalized,

01:39:46.970 --> 01:39:49.200
I feel at home in any language.

01:39:49.200 --> 01:39:50.590
So I'm not a good example.

01:39:50.590 --> 01:39:52.760
I am, to some extent, a monster.

01:39:52.760 --> 01:39:56.390
But teratology being apart,
ordinary people, the ones

01:39:56.390 --> 01:40:00.860
who are very great scholars
and who don't expose themselves

01:40:00.860 --> 01:40:05.390
in the parts of their talk
which are literary discourse,

01:40:05.390 --> 01:40:08.030
linguistic discourse,
are less necessarily

01:40:08.030 --> 01:40:10.670
disfavored as compared
to the ones who

01:40:10.670 --> 01:40:13.070
speak the native tongues,
who are English speakers.

01:40:13.070 --> 01:40:16.680
AUDIENCE: I would actually
argue that the register

01:40:16.680 --> 01:40:21.820
for scientific discourse
becomes a much smaller set

01:40:21.820 --> 01:40:24.140
and becomes a smaller
set that people

01:40:24.140 --> 01:40:27.370
can become confident and
equal because they're not

01:40:27.370 --> 01:40:29.820
really discussing a
very large [INAUDIBLE]..

01:40:29.820 --> 01:40:30.889
HAGEGE: That's right.

01:40:30.889 --> 01:40:31.430
That's right.

01:40:31.430 --> 01:40:33.710
This is an interesting remark.

01:40:33.710 --> 01:40:35.210
And when they do--

01:40:35.210 --> 01:40:37.950
it happens that
they do also discuss

01:40:37.950 --> 01:40:41.250
huge matters and the things
which are not just details,

01:40:41.250 --> 01:40:42.750
what happens?

01:40:42.750 --> 01:40:45.540
AUDIENCE: They go to
their native language.

01:40:45.540 --> 01:40:50.190
HAGEGE: You know, I alluded to
that in the course of my talk,

01:40:50.190 --> 01:40:52.600
that in the 18th
and 19th century,

01:40:52.600 --> 01:40:56.890
many scholars in physics,
chemistry, and other,

01:40:56.890 --> 01:40:59.790
let's just say, [INAUDIBLE]
sciences, were polyglots.

01:40:59.790 --> 01:41:01.470
They were multilinguals.

01:41:01.470 --> 01:41:05.190
I mean, the
possibility of scholars

01:41:05.190 --> 01:41:07.364
being able to speak the
language is not something

01:41:07.364 --> 01:41:08.280
which has not existed.

01:41:08.280 --> 01:41:10.410
It belongs to the
past of Europe.

01:41:10.410 --> 01:41:12.720
And without saying that I
have any nostalgia of that--

01:41:12.720 --> 01:41:14.190
I was not born then--

01:41:14.190 --> 01:41:16.740
knowing that, I think
that this is an example

01:41:16.740 --> 01:41:20.610
probably to be considered
and reflected upon.

01:41:20.610 --> 01:41:24.310
The situation of today
is not quite unavoidable.

01:41:24.310 --> 01:41:27.950
AUDIENCE: Remember
that if a scientist has

01:41:27.950 --> 01:41:32.090
to learn five languages
to get [INAUDIBLE],,

01:41:32.090 --> 01:41:34.840
that becomes a vast barrier
as opposed to having

01:41:34.840 --> 01:41:36.580
to learn just one.

01:41:36.580 --> 01:41:38.080
HAGEGE: To some
extent this is true.

01:41:38.080 --> 01:41:40.750
But this is open to discussion.

01:41:40.750 --> 01:41:42.250
AUDIENCE: I just
want to say one can

01:41:42.250 --> 01:41:45.490
worry about the long-term
implications of the discipline

01:41:45.490 --> 01:41:48.580
when it is conducted
in only one language

01:41:48.580 --> 01:41:53.680
because if we lament the death
of language, of any language,

01:41:53.680 --> 01:41:57.760
as the loss of a whole cultural
system and a way of thinking,

01:41:57.760 --> 01:42:01.590
how much more richer might the
discipline be if, indeed, there

01:42:01.590 --> 01:42:06.874
were multilingual contributions?

01:42:06.874 --> 01:42:07.790
HAGEGE: I quite agree.

01:42:10.310 --> 01:42:12.166
PRESENTER: Maybe one last one.

01:42:12.166 --> 01:42:13.832
AUDIENCE: Do you think
that the language

01:42:13.832 --> 01:42:29.880
of American culture [INAUDIBLE]

01:42:29.880 --> 01:42:32.290
HAGEGE: Every time I
give a public talk,

01:42:32.290 --> 01:42:35.380
there is an Esperantist
somewhere, which

01:42:35.380 --> 01:42:36.505
is not your case, probably.

01:42:36.505 --> 01:42:38.046
AUDIENCE: I don't
speak the language,

01:42:38.046 --> 01:42:40.609
but I've heard many things
about it and don't really know.

01:42:40.609 --> 01:42:41.650
HAGEGE: I have an answer.

01:42:41.650 --> 01:42:43.960
I have an answer because
this is a question on which I

01:42:43.960 --> 01:42:48.680
have striven to think
as much as I can.

01:42:48.680 --> 01:42:52.060
And I must say the
result of this research

01:42:52.060 --> 01:42:56.870
is a great disappointment Dr.
Zamenhof, when he invented it--

01:42:56.870 --> 01:42:59.950
he was a Jew of Bialystok
in Polish, in Poland,

01:42:59.950 --> 01:43:07.930
then occupied by the Czar,
by his white collar Russians.

01:43:07.930 --> 01:43:11.530
And the majority of the
population spoke Polish,

01:43:11.530 --> 01:43:13.620
and the Jews, who
had the ghetto,

01:43:13.620 --> 01:43:15.620
spoke Yiddish and Hebrew--

01:43:15.620 --> 01:43:18.460
did not speak Hebrew but
used it in the prayers.

01:43:18.460 --> 01:43:21.190
So Dr. Zamenhof was
an ophthalmologist,

01:43:21.190 --> 01:43:23.620
as you know, an
oculist, a generous man

01:43:23.620 --> 01:43:27.280
because end of 19th century is
a very internationalist period.

01:43:27.280 --> 01:43:31.060
He thought, I will invent
a language in order to--

01:43:31.060 --> 01:43:35.110
he had the illusion that
a language can lead people

01:43:35.110 --> 01:43:37.720
to a resolution
of conflict, which

01:43:37.720 --> 01:43:40.450
is quite untrue, as I said
in the example of Yugoslavia.

01:43:40.450 --> 01:43:42.430
And he had other
kind of illusions

01:43:42.430 --> 01:43:44.840
which were widely shared
in the end of 19th century.

01:43:44.840 --> 01:43:47.545
So he invented Esperanto.

01:43:47.545 --> 01:43:51.660
In 1887 he published
his manifesto,

01:43:51.660 --> 01:43:55.820
and in 1905 in
Boulogne, in France,

01:43:55.820 --> 01:43:57.700
they had their first
congress international,

01:43:57.700 --> 01:43:59.030
which was a success.

01:43:59.030 --> 01:44:03.160
And then the contemporanes,
the people who lived there

01:44:03.160 --> 01:44:06.880
and who left testimonies,
said that Esperanto

01:44:06.880 --> 01:44:08.890
represented a great hope.

01:44:08.890 --> 01:44:12.040
But now I give you my opinion.

01:44:12.040 --> 01:44:14.950
I think that there
was no impediment,

01:44:14.950 --> 01:44:17.230
there was no obstacle to
the possibility of Esperanto

01:44:17.230 --> 01:44:21.520
becoming a real international
language, all the more since it

01:44:21.520 --> 01:44:24.070
did not have any
political power behind it

01:44:24.070 --> 01:44:25.490
since it was an
invented language.

01:44:25.490 --> 01:44:27.932
So the drawback which
I mentioned as far

01:44:27.932 --> 01:44:30.880
as English is concerned
was not the case then.

01:44:30.880 --> 01:44:34.210
However, my
interpretation is this.

01:44:34.210 --> 01:44:37.810
Unfortunately,
European countries

01:44:37.810 --> 01:44:43.690
were engaged in fratricide,
cruel and human conflicts,

01:44:43.690 --> 01:44:46.040
during less than 50 years.

01:44:46.040 --> 01:44:49.300
And in both occasions,
the massive intervention

01:44:49.300 --> 01:44:52.750
on the United States make
American English what

01:44:52.750 --> 01:44:55.480
I call an Esperanto de facto.

01:44:55.480 --> 01:44:57.370
And this is my interpretation.

01:44:57.370 --> 01:45:03.280
In 1917, President Wilson sent
his troops relatively late,

01:45:03.280 --> 01:45:04.720
but the Americans came.

01:45:04.720 --> 01:45:08.840
In the Second World War,
the American intervention,

01:45:08.840 --> 01:45:11.410
due to Japanese attack,
was much sooner.

01:45:11.410 --> 01:45:16.150
And in both cases, I think that
the advent of American English

01:45:16.150 --> 01:45:19.450
in Europe was the fate,
the bad fate of Esperanto

01:45:19.450 --> 01:45:22.180
because American
English became, itself,

01:45:22.180 --> 01:45:23.910
what I call this
Esperanto de facto.

01:45:23.910 --> 01:45:25.480
And this is one
of the reasons why

01:45:25.480 --> 01:45:27.790
I think the fate of
Esperanto is a sad one

01:45:27.790 --> 01:45:33.390
because human stupidness
and human cruelty

01:45:33.390 --> 01:45:36.240
were the main obstacle
to its diffusion.

01:45:40.440 --> 01:45:42.290
[APPLAUSE]

